{"raw": {"https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000096715449": {"title": "Genetic Pathologist - Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor", "description": "The Precision Medicine Initiative (PMI) and Grafton Clinical Genomics (GCG) at Waipapa Taumata Rau, University of Auckland, are seeking to appoint a Genetic Pathologist to support the clinical accreditation and delivery of next-generation sequencing (NGS) tests in both germline and somatic contexts. This role is a joint opportunity in partnership with Te Toka Tumai Auckland Hospital (Health New Zealand/Te Whatu Ora) . The Diagnostic Genetics department at Te Toka Tumai has established strong links with the University, and this position will work across both organisations to ensure integration of research, clinical service delivery, and teaching:\u00a0 Job Health NZ/Te Whatu Ora .\u00a0 Appointments will be made at Senior Lecturer or Associate Professor level , depending on the successful candidate\u2019s experience and academic profile. You will: Provide clinical oversight for somatic and germline genomic testing, including test validation and sign-out. Partner with GCG to support ISO15189-accredited workflows and accreditation of new assays. Act as the clinical interface with Te Whatu Ora, contributing to patient-centred diagnostic genomics. Contribute to the academic mission of the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, including teaching, postgraduate supervision, and curriculum development. Engage in translational research through the Precision Medicine Initiative, advancing by-New-Zealand-for-New-Zealand approaches to genomics in healthcare. This is an outstanding opportunity to shape the future of genomic medicine in Aotearoa New Zealand, working at the interface of research, clinical practice, and education. Although this position is currently fixed term, there is potential opportunity for tenure with this academic position. You will be a vocationally registered Genetic Pathologist (or eligible for registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand) and hold FRCPA in Genetic Pathology (or equivalent qualification) . Essential requirements: Experience in both somatic and germline genetic pathology . Expertise in NGS test design, validation, and implementation . A strong academic track record in research and/or teaching. Ability to contribute to Molecular Tumour Boards and genetic multidisciplinary team meetings . Excellent communication and collaboration skills, with the ability to work across research, clinical, and academic environments. This is a full-time, 40 hours per week, 24-month fixed term contract. Salary range from $126,863-$158,904 for SL and $165,808 for AP. For more detailed information, please refer to the\u00a0 Position Description . \u00a0 Grafton Clinical Genomics (GCG) is an ISO15189-accredited genomics laboratory hosted by the University of Auckland, operating as a hybrid clinical-research platform under the Auckland Academic Health Alliance. GCG partners closely with Te Toka Tumai Auckland Hospital, ensuring alignment between academic innovation and clinical service. The Precision Medicine Initiative (PMI) is a university-led strategic programme with the goal of enabling genomics and precision medicine in New Zealand. PMI focuses on translational genomics, clinical trials, workforce capability and infrastructure. The Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences is a diverse faculty with more than 4,000 students across six schools, led by internationally recognised researchers. \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities.\u00a0 The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching, and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit\u00a0 Staff Benefits. \u00a0 \nApplications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 18 January 2026\u00a0 to be considered.\u00a0 Please include your cover letter, your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above and contact details of three referees. Please reach out to\u00a0 Professor Peter Browett via p.browett@auckland.ac.nz \u00a0for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n We employ more than 5,400 academic and professional staff to support over 47,000 students, making us one of NZ\u2019s largest employers. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on both our student body and our scholarly community, and we work with both community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the peoples of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to pursuing sustainability.", "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000044404786": {"title": "Expression of Interest - Note takers - All Faculties", "description": "Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please apply online with a cover letter detailing, your current level of study and courses enrolled for in for the current/next semester, an example of your own notes from a class that you have recently taken and your unofficial transcript. We are looking for causal note takers for this coming semester. \u00a0 #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz The University is committed to providing a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment where everyone can thrive. For further information see Te Ara Tautika | the Equity Policy and visit www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Student Disability Services requires note-takers to have demonstrable note-taking skills, excellent communication skills (written and oral) and to have a reasonable level of personal academic achievement. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity To provide the student with a disability a comprehensive set of typed notes within 24 hours of the lecture.\u00a0 These notes are intended for the student to use for their own purpose of study. Content must be factual as per what is presented by the Lecturer and does not contain the authors opinion or comments of the material. Hourly rate is $25.73 per hour and you also accrue 8% holiday pay on a casual employment agreement based on hours submitted for payment. The Note taker must: Be Punctual, reliable, and attend all lectures in person (note taking from recordings are only at the discretion and prior approval from Student Disability staff). Be organised and have the ability to summarise information effectively. Respect the \u00a0privacy\u00a0 of the student.\u00a0 Do not disclose who you are note taking\u00a0for. Type notes in a word document, any exceptions to this must be approved by Student Disability Services Students are encouraged to leave feedback on your notes and you may need to adjust your note taking to meet those requirements Provide notes that are factual and cover the verbal content given by the lecturer, not just re-typing power points or providing a transcript of the lecture. Provide notes that are accessible Clearly structured and well referenced notes are easier to read and support the students learning better than a verbatim record. The benefit to you of being a note taker is that it can improve your own note taking and be an additional skill to add to your CV. Position Description \u00a0 Nga P\u016bkenga Motuhake | Important Skills To be considered for this role you need to be currently enrolled as a student at the University of Auckland. You will ideally be in your 2nd year of study or higher, and hold a GPA of 6 or greater.", "department": "Equity"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000147407329": {"title": "Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies", "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in European Studies, with Gender Studies Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021668 UoA Department Name: Humanities Job Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ranked #47\u00a0in the QS Ranking for Arts & Humanities Ranked 5th in Oceania and 28th globally for sustainability (QS Sustainability Rankings 2026) Shape teaching and research in European literatures and visual cultures, with expertise in Gender Studies The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Situated within Te P\u016btahi M\u0101tauranga | The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna Aronui | Humanities is Aotearoa\u2019s leading centre for humanities scholarship, home to the largest concentration of humanities academics in the country. The School includes English, Drama and Comparative Literature alongside Art History, Asian Studies and Languages, History, Philosophy, Media and Screen Studies, and more. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Lecturer in European Studies, specialising in European literatures and visual cultures, with expertise in Gender Studies. This is a permanent position commencing January 2027, or by negotiation. This is an opportunity to contribute to the development and delivery of European Studies across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, bringing expertise in transnational European contexts across any period from the medieval to the present. You will contribute to core European Studies curriculum, alongside teaching in areas such as French and/or Spanish literatures, comparative literature and drama, film and media. You will also contribute to the Faculty\u2019s cross-disciplinary Gender Studies programme, including teaching the core second-year course in feminist and gender theory on a biennial basis. The role provides scope to develop research and teaching across a broad range of European literary and visual cultures, while contributing to the intellectual life of the School and Faculty. Key areas of contribution include: Teaching and curriculum development across European Studies, French and/or Spanish literatures, comparative literature and drama, film and media, at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. Contributing to the Gender Studies programme, including its core second-year feminist and gender theory course. Developing a productive, high-quality research profile and contributing to the wider research culture of the School. Supervising PhD and other postgraduate students across a broad range of periods and topics. Contributing to academic service, leadership and citizenship within the School, Faculty and wider University. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research and 20% service. Remuneration will be commensurate with experience and achievements and aligned with the University's Academic Standards policy . It will be in the range of\u00a0Lecturer (L): NZ$99,788 - NZ$119,204 per annum. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You will be a collegial academic with expertise in European literatures and visual cultures, and a strong commitment to high-quality research and teaching. You will have excellent communication and collaboration skills and be able to work effectively across disciplinary areas and engage students from diverse cultural backgrounds. You must hold a PhD by the time of appointment in European literatures, comparative literature, film or media studies, languages, cultural studies, Gender Studies or a related field, with: Competency in at least two Romance European languages and cultures represented in our curriculum, preferably French and Spanish. A record of effective tertiary teaching, research and publication in relevant fields. The ability to teach in Gender Studies, with experience in, or readiness to undertake, postgraduate supervision and advising. Expertise in European diasporas, colonialisms and postcolonialisms, with experience adapting and innovating curricula and courses in response to evolving needs, would be desirable. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer As New Zealand\u2019s largest, highest-ranked and most comprehensive research-intensive university, we offer an environment that supports academic excellence and career development, including: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research and Study Leave Research, teaching and leadership development programmes, mentoring and professional development opportunities Flexible employment practices, including hybrid working and flexibility in how and where you work Up to 6.75% employer superannuation scheme Gender-neutral parental leave, including up to nine weeks\u2019 full pay On-site, discounted childcare centres Access to active staff networks and a range of other staff benefits Located in the heart of Auckland, the University offers the opportunity to live and work in one of Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s most vibrant and diverse cities, with easy access to beaches, harbours, native bush and a temperate climate. For more information, please visit our Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by Sunday 04 October, 2026 (11.59pm NZT). Please include a cover letter outlining your interest and suitability for the position, your curriculum vitae, and the names and contact details of three academic referees. For a confidential discussion about the position, including teaching requirements for 2027, please contact Associate Professor Lisa Bailey, Interim Head of School, at [email\u00a0protected] . Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but applications must be made online. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151342338": {"title": "University of Auckland Student Support Adviser", "description": "University of Auckland Student Support Adviser \u2013 M\u0101ori Tauira - Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Student Support Adviser \u2013 M\u0101ori Tauira - Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55569855 UoA Department Name: Student Support and Engagement Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tu\u0101kana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, M\u0101ori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are looking for a Student Support Adviser who is passionate about tauira success, wellbeing, and creating meaningful connections. Based within the Faculty of Science\u2019s Student Support and Engagement team, you will play an important role in supporting M\u0101ori tauira throughout their university journey \u2013 helping navigate university life, connect with people and services, and access opportunities that support their success. This is a relationship-focused role where whanaungatanga and manaakitanga are central to how you work. You will build trusted relationships with tauira and become a visible and approachable source of support within the Faculty. You will work closely with M\u0101ori student networks, Tu\u0101kana, Kai\u0101rahi, and colleagues across the University, helping strengthen connections and ensuring tauira know where to go for support, guidance, and opportunities. Nga P\u016bkenga Motuhake | Important Skills Build genuine and trusted relationships with M\u0101ori tauira and student groups, maintaining a visible presence across the Faculty. Work alongside M\u0101ori support networks, Tu\u0101kana, Kai\u0101rahi and other student groups to promote support, opportunities and services. Take a holistic approach to understanding the needs of individual tauira and help connect them with appropriate wellbeing, academic and co-curricular support. Develop support plans where appropriate and work collaboratively with other University services. Proactively connect with students who may benefit from additional support. Promote and support mentoring, tutoring and study group programmes. Work collaboratively with colleagues across the University Student Wellbeing and Care team to deliver a consistent and positive student experience. Maintain accurate records and follow appropriate processes to ensure effective coordination of student support. Participate in relevant training, including the Marae Model of wellbeing, and contribute to the University\u2019s obligations under the Pastoral Care Code of Practice. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $63,600 to $79,300 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You will bring a strong understanding of te ao M\u0101ori and demonstrated experience working with and alongside M\u0101ori communities. You will be committed to providing culturally responsive support, guidance, and mentorship to students throughout their journey, while building and maintaining meaningful relationships with M\u0101ori wh\u0101nau, iwi, and community networks. You will bring: Minimum 3 years of experience working with students or young people, with an understanding of their diverse needs. Experience working in a customer-focused environment. Strong relationship-building and active listening skills. The ability to support people experiencing moderate levels of distress with empathy, confidence, and professionalism. Excellent oral and written communication skills. An understanding of the University\u2019s responsibilities under Te Tiriti o Waitangi and equity principles. A tertiary qualification or equivalent experience in a relevant discipline, such as social work. Proficiency in te reo M\u0101ori would be an advantage Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and several other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of 18th October 2026, and include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role. If you have any questions about this role, please get in touch with Serena Tiaiti (Student Support & Engagement Mgr) serena.tiaiti@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential discussion.\u00a0Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Te Kura M\u0101tauranga P\u0101ngarau | Department of Mathematics  - 12 months fixed term Auckland, New Zealand Research Fellow - Department of Physics | Te Kura M\u0101tai Ahup\u016bngao \u2013 fixed term - 26 months Auckland, New Zealand Pouako|Lecturer - Earth Science - Te Kura M\u0101tai Taiao - School of Environment Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share. Student Support Adviser \u2013 M\u0101ori Tauira - Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151139328": {"title": "Lecturer in Communication", "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Communication | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Communication Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021555 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Join New Zealand's world-ranked Faculty of Arts and Education (#61 QS Ranking) and contribute to the development of our successful Communications\u00a0programme Inspire our outstanding students and collaborate with highly reputed colleagues Live and work in Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s largest, most cosmopolitan and culturally diverse city Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within the Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna M\u0101rama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description Te Whiwhinga Mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Lecturer in Communication to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral and PhD students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. We particularly welcome and encourage applications from communication scholars with expertise in M\u0101ori and/or Pacific epistemologies and pedagogies, cross-cultural communication, political communication as well as those with existing research and/or teaching relationships with community, public sector and/or industry organisations. This position offers the opportunity to build your academic career within a growing and successful Communication programme. The University of Auckland offers Communication within a specialist Bachelor of Communication, as a major within the Bachelor of Arts, and in Masters and PhD programmes. Researchers and educators in Communication have expertise spanning new media and communication technologies, Indigenous data sovereignty and governance, cultural labour, feminist media studies, social media and digital environments, journalism studies, social change, computational media, technology ethics, gig work and digital and platform economies. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise that adds to or extends the current strengths of the Communication programme. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with M\u0101ori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia- Pacific. It is New Zealand\u2019s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from M\u0101ori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), permanent role (subject to continuation).The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 \u2013 Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience.\u00a0We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. Preferred start date of 1 March 2027. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Monday 26 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email\u00a0protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email\u00a0protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151138560": {"title": "Lecturer in Criminology", "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer in Criminology | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer in Criminology Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021556 UoA Department Name: Social Sciences Company Description Join New Zealand's world-ranked Faculty of Arts and Education (#61 QS Ranking) and contribute to the development of our successful Criminology programme Inspire our outstanding students and collaborate with highly reputed colleagues Live and work in Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s largest, most cosmopolitan and culturally diverse city Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. Situated within The Faculty of Arts and Education, Te Puna M\u0101rama | School of Social Sciences engages critically with studies of culture, politics and issues confronting humanity and society in the past, the present and into the future. Our academics are regarded as experts in their fields. They are at the forefront of Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s academic presence in anthropology, archaeology, communication, criminology, gender studies, global studies, linguistics, international relations, politics, public policy, and sociology. Job Description We are seeking a Lecturer in Criminology to join our team to contribute to undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, supervision of sub-doctoral and PhD students, and to undertake high quality and impactful research. This position offers the opportunity to build your academic career within a dynamic and successful Criminology programme. Criminology has grown significantly over the last ten years and represents one of the most popular majors in the Bachelor of Arts alongside growing postgraduate programmes. Researchers and educators in Criminology at the University of Auckland take broadly critical approaches to the discipline, including an emphasis on Indigenous perspectives, restorative justice, hate crime, cybercrime, penology, gender, policing, prisons, cultural criminology, victimology and justice policies. The successful candidate will have teaching and/or research expertise in one or more of the follow areas: state crime and state harm; white collar crime and/or corporate crime; or green/environmental criminology. A track record of or capacity to engage in public and/or community activism is also desirable. The School of Social Sciences is seeking to grow the number of staff with methodological and theoretical expertise in Indigenous, Kaupapa M\u0101ori, Pacific and/or Asian epistemologies. We are looking for a colleague who can develop successful pedagogical approaches to teaching large undergraduate classes as well as teaching into our growing postgraduate offerings in Criminology. Existing experience in postgraduate supervision or mentoring will be an advantage. The successful candidate will be familiar with or open to learning about Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi) and be prepared to build or extend relationships with M\u0101ori and Pacific communities in Aotearoa New Zealand. The University of Auckland occupies a unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia- Pacific. It is New Zealand\u2019s largest university and an international centre of learning and academic excellence. Auckland is a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and rapidly growing city with a population of 1.8 million, and is the commercial heart of Aotearoa New Zealand. We have an abundance of natural features, including stunning harbours and accessible coastline, native bush and moderate temperatures, which help us to retain our reputation as one of the best cities in the world in which to live. We aspire to contribute to a fair, ethical, and sustainable society, reflecting our commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and our foundational relationship with tangata whenua. The University is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential in an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications from M\u0101ori and Pacific candidates, as well as other applicants that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our links with diverse communities. The workload allocation for this position is 40% teaching, 40% research, and 20% service. This is a full-time (40 hours per week), permanent employment agreement (subject to continuation). The full remuneration range is Lecture step 1: $99,788 \u2013 Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience.\u00a0We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency, and our commitment to pay equity. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description . We are committed to reflecting the diversity of our city and therefore welcome applications from all suitably qualified candidates. Additional Information Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University is committed to supporting your career with: Excellent research, teaching and campus facilities Generous Research & Study Leave Full range of research and leadership development programmes For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 18 October 2026. Please include your cover letter and CV including research and teaching statement, full publication record, teaching record and summarised evaluations, and research grants received. Please identify three referees (who will not be contacted without your express permission). Please reach out to Francis Collins, [email\u00a0protected] (Head of School) or Carisa Showden, [email\u00a0protected] ) (Deputy Head of School) for a confidential conversation. Please note that while we are happy to answer your questions, we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151521319": {"title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer", "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Economics Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55565088 UoA Department Name: Economics Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through its distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching and collaborative partnerships, inspired by its unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. The University is Aotearoa New Zealand\u2019s leading higher education institution and a comprehensive, research-intensive university encompassing six diverse faculties and two research institutes. With more than 7,500 students, the Triple Crown accredited University of Auckland Business School is one of the largest and most successful in the Australasian region. The University of Auckland's position in the top 100 of the 2026 QS World Universities Ranking (65th globally, 1st in New Zealand) confirms both its status as New Zealand's pre-eminent research-led university and its place amongst the top 1% of universities worldwide. The University of Auckland Business School has an unconditional commitment to quality teaching and research in topics that are forward-focused and relevant to the commercial world today. The Department offers comprehensive undergraduate, postgraduate, and PhD programmes. Faculty members are active in publishing in leading international peer-reviewed journals, attracting external grants, supervising doctoral students, and maintaining links with the profession, industry, and government. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking Lecturers who demonstrate strong research potential, as well as a commitment to excellent research-informed teaching that is enhanced by appropriate learning technologies. Continuation (tenure) is based on performance in research, teaching, and service. In addition to growing your academic profile and reputation, you will also be committed to shaping and delivering an exceptional learning experience for students, contributing to an innovative teaching environment using a variety of methods. We invite applications from candidates in any field but are particularly interested in applicants whose work explores areas including Econometrics (Theoretical and Applied) and International Trade. He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you The successful appointee will have a PhD (or be near completion), with demonstrated excellence in research and a commitment to high quality research-informed teaching. You will have a research program that complements and builds upon existing areas of strength within the Department. You will be a highly effective teacher of both undergraduate and graduate students. The appointee will be expected to teach at undergraduate and postgraduate levels in their specialist area, at introductory levels more widely, and to engage in research and publication both personally and through the supervision of research students. You will also be expected to seek research funding, to engage with the profession and academic and non-academic stakeholders, and to contribute to departmental service. Flexibility in teaching and research collaboration within and beyond the Department are essential. All applications should provide the selection committee with: A cover letter and curriculum vitae Any teaching evaluations Job market paper This is a full time, 40 hours per week, permanent employment contract . The salary ranges are (per annum salary depending on experience and qualifications): The full remuneration range is: Lecture step 1: $99,788 \u2013 Lecture step 7: $119,204, depending on qualifications and experience Senior Lecturer - $126,800 - $158,900 NZD, depending on qualifications and experience For more detailed information on the role accountabilities and requirements, please review this position description. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave Research and study leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information please visit Staff Benefits . Additional Information Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply All applications must provide the selection committee with: A cover letter and curriculum vitae Any teaching evaluations Full publication record Job market paper Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 26th October 2026 (NZ time) to be considered. Please highlight\u00a0how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Prasanna Gai -\u00a0Head of Department Economics via [email\u00a0protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151722379": {"title": "University of Auckland General Practitioner", "description": "University of Auckland General Practitioner | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . General Practitioner Part-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 55556474 UoA Department Name: Student Health and Counselling Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are currently seeking an experienced General Practitioners to join our supportive and collaborative team. In this role, you will provide high-quality, patient-centred primary health care to a diverse student population.\u00a0Working within a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, counsellors, and psychologists, you will support students\u2019 physical and mental wellbeing, helping them navigate the unique challenges of university life. This is a rewarding opportunity to work in a wellbeing-focused environment where preventative care, early intervention, and holistic support are at the heart of the service we provide. Your work will make a meaningful difference to students at a pivotal stage of their lives. Please note that we\u00a0offer 20 minute appointments to our patients. Key responsibilities include: Providing skilled health assessment, diagnosis, and treatment services in line with current best practice and professional standards Delivering patient-centred care that supports both physical and mental wellbeing Working collaboratively with nurses, counsellors, and other health professionals to provide integrated care Managing referrals, documentation, follow-up care, and case management in a timely and professional manner Supporting preventative health initiatives, including screening programmes, vaccinations, and health education Providing travel health advice and vaccinations where required Assessing aegrotat applications and providing appropriate academic health advocacy Building strong relationships with internal and external healthcare providers and referral agencies These are permanent positions, and we are flexible with hours \u2014 we welcome applications from both full-time and part-time practitioners, depending on your availability. For more information, please review the Position Description. He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are a compassionate and experienced clinician who enjoys working with young adults and supporting diverse communities. You bring strong clinical judgement, excellent communication skills, and a calm, professional approach to patient care. You will bring: Registration as a medical practitioner in New Zealand with a current NZMC Practising Certificate and indemnity insurance Fellowship of the Royal New Zealand College of General Practitioners. At least two years\u2019 experience in General Practice, ideally including primary or emergency care Current CPR or Advanced Life Support certification Experience working in a multidisciplinary healthcare environment Proven experience working with young adults and diverse communities Confidence using clinical systems such as MedTech or similar practice management software Experience working within a university or student-focused health service would be an advantage but is not essential. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . How to apply\u00a0| Me\u00a0p\u0113hea\u00a0te\u00a0tuku\u00a0tono Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 23/10/2026 to be considered. For any job-specific questions, please reach out to Tania Abernethy-Jones via tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz .\u00a0Please include your cover letter and/or CV highlighting\u00a0how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Innovation Programmes Adviser - M\u0101ori Engagement Auckland, New Zealand Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI Auckland, New Zealand Medical Lead Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share. General Practitioner Auckland, New Zealand Part-time I'm interested I'm interested"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000151722149": {"title": "University of Auckland Medical Lead", "description": "University of Auckland Medical Lead | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Medical Lead Part-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60021163 Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Within Te K\u0101hu Tauira | Student & Scholarly Services, the University Health and Counselling Service (UHCS) provides high-quality, accessible healthcare and wellbeing support to students across the University. Our multidisciplinary team is committed to delivering exceptional care, promoting wellbeing, and helping students thrive throughout their university journey. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an experienced and inspiring Medical Lead to provide clinical leadership and strategic direction within our University Health and Counselling Service. This is a unique opportunity to combine clinical excellence with leadership in a purpose-driven environment that places student wellbeing at the heart of everything we do. As Medical Lead, you will play a key role in shaping the delivery of primary healthcare services for a diverse student population while supporting a team of dedicated clinicians to provide outstanding patient-centred care. Working closely with the General Manager and senior leadership team, you will contribute to service planning, clinical governance, quality improvement initiatives, and the ongoing development of a positive, collaborative, and high-performing clinical culture. This role offers the opportunity to make a meaningful impact on student health outcomes while helping shape the future of healthcare delivery within one of New Zealand\u2019s leading universities. Key responsibilities include: Providing clinical leadership and support to medical practitioners within UHCS Promoting excellence in clinical practice, patient safety, and quality improvement initiatives Supporting clinical governance activities and ensuring compliance with relevant professional standards and legislation Contributing to service planning, operational decision-making, and strategic initiatives Working collaboratively with multidisciplinary teams including nurses, counsellors, psychologists, and administrative staff Supporting workforce development, mentoring, and professional growth within the medical team Maintaining a high standard of clinical care through direct patient consultations Supporting the development and implementation of evidence-based healthcare practices Building strong relationships with internal stakeholders and external healthcare providers Contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and student-centred service delivery This role combines clinical leadership with hands-on practice, allowing you to continue delivering care while helping drive service excellence and positive health outcomes for students. This is a full-time , 37.5 hours per week role on a permanent contract. For more information, please review the Position Description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are an experienced and respected medical practitioner who is passionate about delivering high-quality healthcare and supporting the development of others. You bring strong clinical leadership skills, sound judgement, and the ability to foster positive relationships across multidisciplinary teams. You are committed to continuous improvement, patient-centred care, and creating an inclusive and supportive environment where both students and colleagues can thrive. Most importantly, you are motivated by the opportunity to make a meaningful contribution to student wellbeing while helping shape the future direction of a trusted and highly valued health service. Ng\u0101 p\u016bkenga motuhake | Important skills and experience Current registration with the Medical Council of New Zealand and a current practising certificate Significant experience working in General Practice or primary healthcare Demonstrated clinical leadership experience and the ability to support and mentor clinicians Strong understanding of clinical governance, quality improvement, and patient safety principles Excellent communication and relationship-building skills Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary healthcare teams A commitment to culturally responsive practice and equitable health outcomes The following would be beneficial: Vocational registration in General Practice Experience working with young adults or within tertiary health services Experience leading service improvement and healthcare innovation initiatives Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland provides a supportive, people-centred working environment where your wellbeing and professional development are genuinely valued. As part of our team, you\u2019ll enjoy: Flexible employment practices Up to 6.75% employer superannuation contribution Five weeks\u2019 annual leave A wide range of professional development opportunities Generous parental leave provisions Access to staff discounts, recreation facilities, wellbeing initiatives, and discounted parking The opportunity to combine clinical practice with leadership in a rewarding university healthcare environment The chance to make a lasting impact on the health, wellbeing, and success of thousands of students Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online to be considered. Please submit your application by 23 October 2026 . Please include your cover letter and CV outlining how your skills and experience align with this role. For a confidential conversation about the role, please contact Tania Abernethy-Jones via tania.abernethy-jones@auckland.ac.nz. Please note that applications sent via email will not be accepted. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Postdoctoral Fellow - Professor in MRI Auckland, New Zealand General Practitioner Auckland, New Zealand Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share. Medical Lead Auckland, New Zealand Part-time I'm interested I'm interested"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152036579": {"title": "University of Auckland Development Manager", "description": "University of Auckland Development Manager - Fundraising  | SmartRecruiters Sorry, Internet Explorer 11 is no longer supported by SmartRecruiters Please update to one of the following browsers: Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox You can find details about supported web browsers here . Development Manager - Fundraising Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 00016813 UoA Department Name: Alumni Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Development Manager to fundraise and partner with our Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences (FMHS) and institutes, connecting world-leading research, teaching and health initiatives with individual donors, corporate partners and philanthropic organisations. Together, these organisations form a nationally significant health, research and innovation portfolio dedicated to improving health outcomes for New Zealand and beyond. Bringing together expertise across medicine, health sciences, engineering and technology, they support groundbreaking research, transformative education and collaborative partnerships that address some of society's most pressing health challenges. As part of the Alumni Relations and Development team, you will manage a portfolio of major gift prospects and supporters, building strong relationships and developing tailored engagement strategies that lead to significant philanthropic investment. Working closely with the Dean, senior academic leaders, researchers and colleagues across the University, you will help identify opportunities that inspire donors to support transformative research, student success and innovation. This is a highly relationship-focused fundraising role that combines strategy, donor engagement and major gift development. You will guide prospects through the full donor journey, from identification and cultivation through to solicitation and stewardship, building trusted relationships with high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations and corporate partners. Success in this role comes from curiosity, initiative, strong commercial acumen and the ability to connect donors with opportunities that create meaningful and lasting impact. Your responsibilities will include: Managing a portfolio of major gift prospects, trusts, foundations and corporate partners, developing strategies to secure significant philanthropic support Building strong relationships with donors, faculty leadership, researchers and University stakeholders to identify and advance funding opportunities Developing donor engagement plans, proposals and stewardship activities that foster long-term relationships and investment Working alongside the Dean and senior leaders to prioritise prospects and align fundraising activity with faculty priorities Supporting the development and implementation of faculty fundraising strategies, plans and performance measures Collaborating with colleagues across Alumni Relations and Development to deliver coordinated donor engagement and fundraising initiatives This is a permanent, full-time position ( 37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $118,000 to $128,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role is provided in the position description linked HERE He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are an experienced fundraiser or relationship manager with a track record of building meaningful, long-term relationships that lead to significant financial investment. You are comfortable engaging with high-net-worth individuals, philanthropic trusts, foundations and senior corporate stakeholders, and understand how to move relationships from initial engagement through to successful solicitation and stewardship. You are naturally curious and enjoy understanding people's motivations, interests and aspirations. You know how to develop trust, identify alignment between donor interests and organisational priorities, and create compelling opportunities for support. Equally comfortable working independently and collaboratively, you thrive in a relationship-driven environment where success comes from strategic engagement, persistence and excellent stakeholder management. E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking Demonstrated success securing major gifts, philanthropic investment or high-value commercial partnerships through relationship-based engagement Experience managing a portfolio of high-net-worth individuals, trusts, foundations, corporate partners or key strategic stakeholders Proven ability to identify, cultivate, solicit and steward supporters through a structured relationship management or moves-management process Exceptional relationship-building and influencing skills, with confidence engaging senior leaders, donors and external stakeholders Demonstrable commercial acumen, with experience developing proposals, delivering presentations and achieving revenue or fundraising targets A relevant tertiary qualification, or equivalent experience, combined with a genuine interest in supporting world-leading medical research and health education Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on\u2011site amenities Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, 18\u00a0October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant, via neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice Cookies Settings I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Other jobs at University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management Auckland, New Zealand Group Services Administrator - Permanent and Fixed Term Auckland, New Zealand Health Economist Auckland, New Zealand Show all jobs Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share. Development Manager - Fundraising Auckland, New Zealand Full-time I'm interested I'm interested"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152099717": {"title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer", "description": "University of Auckland Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Health Leadership and Health Management Part-time Campus: Grafton Campus Position Number: 60022264 UoA Department Name: Health Systems Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting more than 46,000 students, we are one of New Zealand\u2019s largest employers. Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Population Health, within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, is seeking a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Health Leadership and Health Management. This is an opportunity to contribute to the development of future health leaders while advancing research and scholarship that supports the evolving needs of the health sector in Aotearoa New Zealand. Working across both the Master of Health Leadership and Bachelor of Health Sciences programmes, you will join a collaborative academic environment committed to improving health outcomes, reducing inequities, and strengthening health systems. You will work alongside experienced colleagues and sector partners to shape high-quality teaching, research, and engagement activities, while contributing to the future direction of health leadership education and scholarship. Key responsibilities Lead and contribute to teaching within Health Leadership and Health Management programmes. Design and deliver contemporary, research-informed learning and assessment. Supervise postgraduate student research and support student success. Undertake a programme of high-quality research relevant to health leadership and management in Aotearoa New Zealand. Develop and maintain strong relationships across the health sector. Contribute to programme development and the strategic direction of the Master of Health Leadership. Participate in service and leadership activities across the School, Faculty, and wider University community. This opportunity is for a permanent role, part time for 32 hours per week. The salary range for each academic level are: Lecturer: $99,788 - $119,204 (fulltime equivalent) Senior Lecturer: $126,863 - $145,741 (fulltime equivalent) Your salary will be determined based on your experience, qualifications, and the level at which you are appointed. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You will be an engaged academic or experienced sector leader with a strong interest in developing the next generation of health leaders. You bring a collaborative approach, excellent communication skills, and a commitment to working across diverse communities and organisations. You will be motivated by opportunities to connect scholarship with real-world impact and will value inclusive, culturally responsive approaches to teaching, research, and engagement. A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its significance within the health sector is essential. You will bring: PhD in a relevant discipline such as health management, health leadership, public health, health systems, health policy, management, or organisational studies. Experience in leadership and/or management within the health or social sector. Demonstrated research capability in health leadership or health management. Understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its relevance to health. Ability to build and maintain relationships across the healthcare sector. Excellent organisational, communication, and collaborative skills. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home if feasible, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 27 October 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Professor Monique Jonas, via [email\u00a0protected] , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. #LI-DNI Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152759298": {"title": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser", "description": "University of Auckland Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Schools and Community Engagement Adviser | Te Terenga Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60019816 UoA Department Name: Marketing and Recruitment Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific. Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Are you a confident communicator and relationship builder who knows how to engage ambitious young people? We\u2019re looking for a Schools and Community Engagement Adviser to join our high-performing team and help strengthen the University\u2019s connections with secondary schools, students, wh\u0101nau, and school communities. This is more than an outreach role. You\u2019ll be part of the University\u2019s student recruitment journey, helping attract high-achieving students and positioning UoA as a university of choice. Our Schools and Community Engagement approach is grounded in Manaakitanga, Whanaungatanga, and Kaitiakitanga \u2014 caring for people, building lasting relationships, and taking responsibility for the communities we work with. What you\u2019ll be doing You\u2019ll take ownership of an assigned portfolio of schools and become a trusted first point of contact for students, teachers, careers advisers, and school communities. Your days will be varied. You could be: Building strong relationships with schools and careers advisers and understanding what matters to their students. Representing at school visits, information evenings, events, and other engagement opportunities. Delivering confident, engaging presentations to students, teachers, and school leaders. Supporting the attraction and engagement of high-achieving secondary students, including students considering Cambridge and IB pathways. Using application, enrolment and engagement data to identify trends, opportunities and areas of focus. Developing tailored resources, digital content and engagement activities for different student audiences. Helping organise and deliver major University events and initiatives \u2014 and being someone who is comfortable rolling up their sleeves to make them happen. Working across the University with faculties, communications and marketing, student services and other teams to create a connected prospective student experience. The University of Auckland Contributing to projects and improvements that make it easier and more engaging for students to explore their future at The University of Auckland . Providing accurate, timely and genuinely useful advice to students and their wh\u0101nau as they navigate their options. The role combines relationship management, recruitment, presentations, events, project work, data and strategy **Ideal start date January 2027** This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $73,400 to $90,700 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You\u2019re someone who can build rapport quickly, but you\u2019re also comfortable holding your own. You\u2019re confident, personable and strategic enough to see the bigger picture. You\u2019ll bring: Excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills. Strong presentation and facilitation skills, with the confidence to engage a room full of young people. A natural ability to build and maintain genuine professional relationships. The judgement to understand different audiences and tailor your approach accordingly. Strong organisation skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities, events and projects. A practical, solutions-focused approach \u2014 you see something that could work better and look for ways to improve it. Confidence working with data, systems and digital tools, with the ability to pick up new technology quickly. An analytical mindset and the ability to turn information and trends into useful insights. A strong customer and stakeholder focus, while maintaining appropriate professional boundaries. The confidence to work strategically and contribute ideas, rather than simply waiting for instructions. Must hold a full, clean New Zealand drivers licence Experience working within the New Zealand secondary school sector and an understanding of the tertiary environment would be valuable. Experience working with academically focused schools or students following Cambridge or IB programmes would be an advantage. An understanding of te ao M\u0101ori and confidence engaging respectfully with M\u0101ori and Pacific communities would also be highly valued. A commitment to equity and inclusion is important to how we engage with young people and their wh\u0101nau. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: \u00b7 Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) \u00b7 Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme \u00b7 A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 23rd October 2026, to be considered. We may close this advert early if we have a large number of applications. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to\u00a0Isabella Mory (Scholars Outreach & Engagement Manager) via [email\u00a0protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153439719": {"title": "Professional Teaching Fellow", "description": "University of Auckland Professional Teaching Fellow - School of Biological Sciences | Te Kura M\u0101tauranga Koiora | SmartRecruiters Google Chrome Microsoft Edge Apple Safari Mozilla Firefox Professional Teaching Fellow - School of Biological Sciences | Te Kura M\u0101tauranga Koiora Full-time Campus: Auckland City Campus Position Number: 60022092 UoA Department Name: Biological Sciences Company Description Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tu\u0101kana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, M\u0101ori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae . Job Description Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are inviting applications to join our team in the School of Biological Sciences/Te Kura M\u0101tauranga Koiora (SBS). as a permanent academic staff member in the position of Professional Teaching Fellow (1.0 FTE \u2013 PTF Level 1\u20132 on the University Pay Scale). The successful candidate will contribute to our teaching programmes, including the Tertiary Foundation Certificate and undergraduate programmes. The role includes teaching lectures, laboratories, field trips and tutorials, as well as curriculum development, resource design and academic pastoral care. You will also contribute to service-related activities across the University and wider community. In the future, opportunities may exist to teach at international partner institutions, including in China, for periods of approximately one month at a time. He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you We are looking for a candidate who is passionate about delivering impactful, research-informed and student-centred learning experiences. You will work within a collegial and collaborative academic community, coordinating undergraduate courses and delivering high-quality, accessible teaching using evidence-based pedagogical practices. Staff at the University of Auckland are expected to contribute to upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi in their work and to enact the principles outlined in Taumata Teitei, the University\u2019s strategic plan. A postgraduate tertiary qualification in biology is required, with subject expertise in at least one of the following areas: Ecology, Biodiversity, Evolution or Quantitative Biology. A track record of developing innovative teaching methodologies and high-quality learning resources is preferred, as is familiarity with different teaching modalities and learning management systems. Experience coordinating undergraduate courses is advantageous, and a teaching qualification is preferred but not essential. The role involves providing pastoral care to students with diverse learning needs, as well as contributing to other relevant teaching and academic activities across undergraduate levels. Candidates are expected to demonstrate engagement with, or a strong interest in contributing to, the scholarship of teaching and learning. We are looking for someone who demonstrates a strong commitment to inclusiveness and has experience teaching students from diverse backgrounds, particularly M\u0101ori and Pacific students. Skills that support the inclusion of te ao M\u0101ori principles within programmes, teaching and the student experience are highly desirable. For more information on the role, please view the Position Description This is a full-time (40 hrs per week), permanent role. The remuneration is from\u00a0 $105,790 (PTF1) to $ (PTF2) 116,929 per annum, and will be dependent on skills and experience M\u014d t\u014d m\u0101tou r\u014dp\u016b | About the team SBS is a large, lively and interdisciplinary School delivering excellence in research and teaching, with strong international and community links. We are based in the heart of Auckland/T\u0101maki Makaurau, a harbourside city renowned for its rich cultural diversity, beaches, islands, iconic volcanoes and lush forests. Auckland is consistently ranked among the world\u2019s top cities for quality of life. SBS is home to the University of Auckland\u2019s first and oldest Tu\u0101kana programme, established in 1991, which focuses on enhancing the academic learning success of M\u0101ori and Pacific students. Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is committed to a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential within an inclusive and collaborative community of scholars. We welcome applications that will enhance the diversity of our staff and strengthen our connections with diverse communities. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through flexible employment practices and a culture that encourages and supports staff to reach their full potential. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks\u2019 annual leave, a company superannuation scheme, discounted car parking, childcare, and a range of other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit our Staff Benefits page. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply We welcome applications from candidates who bring a range of experience, skills, and perspectives. If you meet most of the requirements and are excited about the opportunity to contribute to our teaching and student experience, we encourage you to apply. All applications must be submitted online and include a combined cover letter and curriculum vitae, evidence of qualifications, a statement outlining your approach to teaching and/or teaching philosophy.and contact information, including email addresses, for three referees.\u00a0Please note that letters of reference may be requested from longlisted candidates. Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 31st October 2026 to be considered. Please reach out to Associate Professor David Goldstone (Head of School -Biological Sciences) via [email\u00a0protected] for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email Additional Information Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to www.equity.auckland.ac.nz By clicking the link above or any third-party link within this posting, you are leaving this site and going to a third-party website where the third-party website's terms and privacy policy apply I'm interested I'm interested Privacy Notice I'm interested Refer a friend share this job Share to WeChat \u00d7 Copy the link and open WeChat to share. Copy to clipboard Open WeChat Share to WeChat \u00d7 Use Scan QR Code in WeChat and click \u00b7\u00b7\u00b7 to share."}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154346208": {"title": "Rainbow Community Engagement Assistant \u2013 Health Research (casual) Multiple positions available ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Multiple positions available across Whang\u0101rei, Auckland, Hamilton, Tauranga, Wellington & Dunedin Are you passionate about working with diverse communities and interested in contributing to health research that can make a real difference? We have multiple casual positions available across Aotearoa New Zealand, giving you the opportunity to gain hands-on experience engaging with members of rainbow communities as part of population health research. You\u2019ll join the Sex and Prevention of Transmission Study (SPOTS) team, helping to recruit participants \u2014 primarily men who have sex with men (MSM) \u2014 to complete a research survey. The study aims to generate valuable insights that can contribute to improved HIV prevention and blood donation policy in Aotearoa New Zealand. We\u2019re looking for outgoing, approachable and organised people who are confident communicating with people from diverse backgrounds and enjoy building positive connections. Experience in a customer-facing, community or healthcare setting would be highly desirable, as would experience or knowledge of rainbow communities. In this role, you\u2019ll help our team engage with and enrol participants at local Pride events and community venues, supporting data collection across Aotearoa New Zealand. As this is a casual position, there are no fixed hours. We anticipate approximately 8\u201320 hours in total, including training. This could be an ideal opportunity for a student seeking valuable paid research and community engagement experience alongside their studies. Salary: $30 per hour \nStart date: 1 February 2027 \nEnd date: 30 April 2027 \nHours: Approximately 8\u201320 hours in total, including full training Availability: You must be available Monday to Sunday, with availability mainly on Thursday to Sunday, especially in the evenings and on weekends. As part of your application, please confirm your availability during the peak study period (February to April). For more detailed information, please refer to the position description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you We welcome applications from people of all genders, sexual identities, backgrounds and skillsets. You\u2019ll ideally have: Experience working in a people-facing role, such as customer service, volunteering, community engagement or a similar setting. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the confidence to engage with people from diverse backgrounds. An interest in health research and improving health outcomes for rainbow communities. A professional, approachable and respectful manner when engaging with research participants. Your application will stand out if you also have experience working with rainbow communities, have provided health or community services, or are currently studying or training in a health-related field. If you\u2019re looking for an opportunity to contribute to an important health research study, connect with diverse communities, and gain valuable practical experience, we\u2019d love to hear from you. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities.\u00a0 The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.\u00a0 Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 7th November 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV, highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above, and your preferred location Please reach out to Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.\u00a0 Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .", "department": ""}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154345888": {"title": "Rainbow Community Testing Assistant \u2013 Health Research - 3 months - Multiple positions available", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Multiple positions available across Auckland, Wellington & Christchurch Are you a reliable, people-focused person with excellent interpersonal skills and a high level of empathy and confidentiality? We have multiple Tester positions available across Aotearoa New Zealand, offering an opportunity to play an important role in a population health research study. You\u2019ll join the Sex and Prevention of Transmission Study (SPOTS) team, working directly with participants (primarily men who have sex with men (MSM)) at venues in your local area. You\u2019ll be responsible for supporting participants through the testing process, providing a welcoming and respectful experience while maintaining a high level of professionalism, confidentiality and care. This is a people-focused role where your ability to build trust, communicate sensitively and make people feel comfortable will be essential. Testers will be based onsite at participating venues and will need to be available to work across Monday to Sunday, including daytime and some evening shifts. Some flexibility to be on call may also be required. We\u2019re looking for reliable, approachable and empathetic people who are comfortable working with diverse communities and can maintain full confidentiality when handling sensitive information. Salary: $35 per hour \n Start date: 1 February 2027 \n End date: 30 April 2027 \n Availability: Full-time availability across Monday to Sunday, including some evenings and the ability to be on call when required \n Location: Onsite at participating venues Training will be provided , and you\u2019ll receive full support from the SPOTS team throughout the study. You don\u2019t need to have previous experience as a Tester \u2014 we\u2019re looking for people with the right attitude, interpersonal skills and commitment to providing a positive participant experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you We welcome applications from people of all genders, sexual identities, backgrounds and skillsets. To be successful in this role, you will have: Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage respectfully with a diverse range of people. A high level of empathy, discretion and confidentiality when working with participants and sensitive information. A professional, approachable and non-judgemental manner. Strong attention to detail and the ability to follow processes and instructions accurately. A high level of reliability and commitment, including the flexibility to work across different days and times. Confidence working independently at venues while also being part of a wider research team. The ability to remain calm, supportive and professional in sensitive or potentially challenging situations. Experience working in a healthcare, community, customer-facing, research or other people-focused environment would be advantageous but is not essential as full training and support will be provided. Experience working with rainbow communities or an understanding of the communities involved in the research would also be highly desirable. If you\u2019re a reliable, empathetic and people-focused individual who would enjoy making a meaningful contribution to health research in Aotearoa, we\u2019d love to hear from you. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer \nThe University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university and maintains significant computational, laboratory and analytic facilities. Auckland itself is frequently rated as one of the world\u2019s most liveable cities.\u00a0 The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme \n\u2022\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.\u00a0 Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date of 7th November 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV, highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above, and your preferred location Please reach out to Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.\u00a0 Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .", "department": ""}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154063048": {"title": "Research Fellow -  Te Whare Takiura M\u0101tai P\u016btaiao Moana | Institute of Marine Science \u2013 24 month fixed term ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking an outstanding and highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join an interdisciplinary research programme focused on enhancing the restoration potential of ecologically important shellfish species. Shellfish restoration is a critical tool for rebuilding marine ecosystems, improving water quality, enhancing biodiversity, and supporting coastal resilience. However, restoration outcomes vary considerably among species due to differences in life-history traits, dispersal dynamics, habitat requirements, population connectivity, and environmental sensitivities. This position will focus on applying network theory to identify the barriers limiting shellfish restoration and the recovery of ecosystem function. The successful candidate will integrate with a wider collaborative research team working at the interface of ecology, conservation, restoration science, and quantitative modelling. Research Focus The postdoctoral fellow will: Deploy ecological network models to marine restoration systems using experimental and observational datasets. Identify the ecological mechanisms and feedbacks that facilitate or constrain shellfish recovery. Work collaboratively with ecologists, restoration practitioners, iwi, community groups, and government agencies. Publish high-impact research in international journals and present findings at scientific conferences. Contribute to mentoring postgraduate students and fostering a collaborative research environment. For more information on the role, please view the\u00a0 Position Description This is a full-time (40 hrs per week), 24-month fixed-term role The remuneration is $99,788 - $103,024 per annum, dependent on skills and experience He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you We are particularly interested in candidates who are interested in applying network approaches to investigate how species interactions, habitat characteristics, population connectivity, environmental conditions, and ecosystem feedbacks influence restoration success. Applicants should have: A PhD in ecology, marine science, environmental science, applied mathematics, complex systems, or a related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in network analysis and/or ecological modelling. Strong quantitative and computational skills (e.g., R, Python, Bayesian modelling, graph theory, statistical modelling). A strong publication record relative to career stage. Excellent communication skills and collaboration skills. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer This position provides an opportunity to contribute to high-impact research addressing pressing challenges in marine ecosystem restoration. The successful candidate will work within a supportive and collaborative research environment with access to leading researchers, extensive datasets, and opportunities for professional development. We welcome applications from candidates with diverse backgrounds and perspectives and are committed to fostering an inclusive and supportive workplace. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.\u00a0 Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the closing date, 6th November 2026 , to be considered Applicants should submit: A cover letter describing their interest in the position and relevant experience. A curriculum vitae. Contact details for at least two academic referees. Examples of relevant publications or research outputs. An indication of when they would be able to start if successful. Please reach out to Jenny Hillman\u00a0(Senior Lecturer - Marine Science) via\u00a0 j.hillman@auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science\u00a0 at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tu\u0101kana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, M\u0101ori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae .", "department": "Faculty of Science"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153655739": {"title": "Casual Study Doctor \u2013 Neurogenetics", "description": "M\u014d t\u014d m\u0101tou r\u014dp\u016b | About the team The Neurogenetic Research Clinic, part of the Centre for Brain Research, is a leading centre for clinical research in rare neurological disorders. Our work spans international clinical trials, natural history studies and translational research aimed at improving diagnosis, treatment and quality of life for people living with inherited neurological conditions. Our multidisciplinary team is led by Associate Professor Richard Roxburgh, Consultant Neurologist, and includes neurophysiologists, research nurses, physiotherapists, genetic counsellors and study coordinators. Participant assessments are conducted at Clinical Research Centre (Building 507, Grafton) for research in collaboration with international partners and pharmaceutical sponsors. Current projects include: Phase 3 clinical trials for focal epilepsy (tablets) and myotonic dystrophy (infusion). Observational studies to validate clinical outcome measures, characterise phenotypes and identify biomarkers. Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a motivated doctor to join our team as a Sub-Investigator on a casual basis. This is a varied and engaging role that offers the opportunity to advance your clinical research career by contributing to cutting-edge clinical research, improving the lives of people living with rare disorders, and developing expertise in neurological assessment. Following training in specific neurological tests, the role will involve conducting assessments following study protocols, including (but not limited to): Obtaining informed consent and conducting participant eligibility assessments. Taking medical histories and reviewing concomitant medications. Performing protocol-specific physical and neurological examinations. Conducting and documenting study assessments in accordance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP). Monitoring participant safety, including review of laboratory results and ECGs. Communicating clinically relevant findings with participants and their healthcare providers. Performing protocol-specific procedures where trained and required including (but not limited to) venepuncture, rating scales (requiring certification) and common/bespoke study software. Collaborating with study coordinators, allied health professionals and international research partners. Maintaining GCP qualifications and any other qualifications required for studies. Responding to queries raised by study monitors. For clinical trials, additional tasks include: Checking investigational products (IPs) and supervising administration where required. Responding to concerns raised by the Research Nurse that arise during IP infusion including being available to attend emergency calls. Judging whether events are Adverse Events or Serious Adverse reactions and taking appropriate management decisions to ensure participant\u2019s wellbeing. He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you Our ideal candidate will bring: Essential Current registration and practising certificate with the Medical Council of New Zealand. Minimum three years' postgraduate clinical experience. Excellent clinical assessment and communication skills. Strong organisational skills and attention to detail. Ability to work independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team. Current professional indemnity insurance and up to date CPR. Desirable Experience in General Practice or Neurology. Interest in or experience with clinical research. Current GCP certification. Experience with neurological examination and assessment. Experience working with people living with chronic neurological disorders. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of \u00a025 October 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Sarah Nagar, via sarah.nagar@auckland.ac.nz , for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and a number of professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. Our lecturers and researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students.", "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000153621769": {"title": "Practice Nurse", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a Practice Nurse to join our general practice nursing team working across our Grafton and City Campuses. Our clinics specifically support our students and are not open to the general public. Ideally, we are looking for a nurse with \u00a0 experience working in a multidisciplinary health setting with proven clinical experience with young adults and diverse communities. This would be a great role for someone with an interest in sexual health, mental health, and a supportive wellness approach. The role is full-time, 37.5 hours per week, on a permanent basis.\u00a0 Rostered hours are:\u00a0Monday 08:00-18.00, Tuesday- Thursday 0800-17.00 and Friday 08.00-14.20. We offer a competitive salary, which is bolstered by our Kiwi Saver/Unisaver contributions, which can match up to 6.75% of your salary. On top of this, we also have\u00a025 days\u00a0of annual leave a year, with a\u00a0Christmas shutdown\u00a0to ensure we all get a good summer break. The salary range for this role is $73,400 - $90,700 prorated per annum, depending on experience and qualifications. More information about this role is available in the Position Description.\u00a0 \n M\u014d t\u014d m\u0101tou r\u014dp\u016b | About the team You will join a nursing team of eight managed by a Nurse Lead and supported by an effective administrative team. We offer a close-knit team environment, and the wider nursing team allows for a positive social feeling and helps us all to get the best outcomes for our patients. For more information on our health services, click\u00a0 here . \u00a0 He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you To succeed in this role, you will be a registered nurse interested in sexual health, mental health, and ideally experienced within a general practice. You will be motivated to provide quality service and enjoy working collaboratively with the wider team to achieve this. It is important that you have an Independent Authorised Vaccinator Certificate\u00a0or be willing to take the training course to be qualified. This is a great opportunity for someone who enjoys a varied day, can work comfortably with a wide range of people, and is keen to contribute to health and wellbeing in the New Zealand tertiary sector. \u00a0 E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking A\u00a0Registered Nurse\u00a0in New Zealand with a current Practising Certificate Someone with an\u00a0Independent Vaccinator Authorisation Certificate\u00a0or willing to train towards this Ideally, you will be qualified to take Cervical smears (preferable) You will have a current validated CPR and/or Advanced Life Support Certification Are able to show proven clinical experience with young adults and diverse communities Someone who can demonstrate experience with health screening and healthy lifestyle interventions You will need to consider yourself to have a high level of cultural competence \u00a0 Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer We are proud of providing an environment that enables your natural abilities and rewards your hard work. We offer benefits and support throughout your career that allows you to develop and grow. We are committed to providing a supportive working environment through: An excellent general practice, that\u2019s growing and progressive A flexible and supportive environment A hard-working yet fun-loving culture A competitive salary with\u00a05 weeks annual leave. With the option to buy and sell additional leave The option to have your\u00a0superannuation matched at 6.75%\u00a0via our two pension schemes Flexible employment practices. Including adaptive working hours A generous\u00a0parental leave\u00a0policy An established\u00a0internal mobility programme\u00a0(Internal First) which offers our staff real options to change their career and try out new ways of working In addition, we offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, on-site childcare options, leisure and recreation facilities, and a number of other discounted products and services.\u00a0 For more information, please visit\u00a0 Staff Benefits . \u00a0 Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply \n \nApplications must be submitted online by the closing date of 3 November 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. \n \nWe will be reviewing applications as they come in, and may close the advert early.\u00a0 Please reach out to Kiri Mokomoko - kiri.mokomoko@auckland.ac.nz ,\u00a0for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.", "department": "Student and Scholarly Services"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152582719": {"title": "Senior Project Manager \u2013 Planning & Development", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is an opportunity to lead the planning and shaping of major capital projects that will influence the future of the University of Auckland's campus environment. As a Senior Project Manager within Campus Environment, you will take a leadership role across a diverse portfolio of complex capital projects and programmes. With a strong focus on the front end of project delivery, you will guide projects through feasibility, business case development, stakeholder engagement, design development and governance processes, ensuring projects are positioned for long-term success. Working within a large and diverse organisation, you will partner with academic leaders, service divisions, consultants, contractors and external stakeholders to translate strategic objectives into practical and achievable project outcomes. This role requires someone who can confidently navigate competing priorities, support decision-making at senior levels and build consensus across a broad stakeholder group. This is a unique opportunity to contribute to high-profile new build and refurbishment projects across teaching, research and student environments. Current and future projects span a range of complex environments, including research facilities, laboratories, libraries and student-facing spaces, supporting the University's long-term campus development objectives. Your responsibilities will include: Leading projects through feasibility, business case development, planning, design and procurement phases Managing consultant teams and supporting effective governance, decision-making and stakeholder engagement processes Developing business cases, project briefs and strategic recommendations that support investment decisions Building strong relationships with stakeholders across faculties, service divisions, consultants and external partners Managing project budgets, risks, programmes and approvals to ensure successful delivery outcomes Supporting construction delivery, commissioning and handover activities where required This is a permanent full-time position ( 37.5 hours per week). The salary range for this role is $168,000 to $182,000 , negotiable depending on skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you Ideally, you are an experienced client-side or consultancy project professional, although candidates from other backgrounds will also be considered. You will have a proven track record of establishing and leading complex capital projects within large organisations. While you understand the full project lifecycle, your strengths lie in the early phases of project delivery, where stakeholder engagement, strategic planning, business case development, and design leadership are critical to success. You are astute and comfortable working across complex governance environments. You know how to bring diverse stakeholder groups together, manage competing priorities and build support for project outcomes. You can confidently challenge ideas, ask the right questions and influence decision-making while maintaining strong relationships. You bring a balanced approach to design, cost control and delivery. You appreciate the importance of creating high-quality environments without losing sight of budget, programme and operational requirements. Experience working in complex institutional, government, tertiary education, research or healthcare environments would be highly regarded. E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking Extensive experience establishing and leading major capital projects or programmes within complex, multi-stakeholder environments Strong capability in business case development, project planning, design management and consultant leadership Demonstrated experience working within formal governance structures and presenting to senior leadership and decision-making groups Exceptional stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to influence, negotiate and build consensus across diverse groups Proven experience managing project budgets, procurement, contracts, risks and approvals processes A relevant tertiary qualification in project management, construction, engineering, architecture, property or a related discipline Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on\u2011site amenities Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, 18 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Neil Gardner, Senior Recruitment Consultant at neil.gardner@auckland.ac.nz \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz", "department": "Campus Environment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000152547998": {"title": "Kaiako - K\u014dhanga Reo | Early Childhood Education", "description": "Te whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Ehara taku toa i te toa takitahi, he toa takitini My success is not that of an individual, but that of many Join Hineteiwaiwa K\u014dhanga Reo , a unique and special learning environment at Waipapa Taumata Rau. Hineteiwaiwa is a teacher-led K\u014dhanga Reo where te reo M\u0101ori, tikanga M\u0101ori, and whanaungatanga are at the heart of everyday learning. We are seeking passionate Kaiako who are committed to nurturing mokopuna, empowering wh\u0101nau, and contributing to an environment where te reo M\u0101ori is lived, valued, and celebrated. This is an opportunity to join a warm, collaborative t\u012bma and make a meaningful difference in the lives of tamariki while continuing to grow your own knowledge and practice within a strong kaupapa M\u0101ori setting. We welcome applications from candidates seeking either full-time or part-time opportunities. Licensed for 25 tamariki aged 7 months to 6 years, our service benefits from exceptional teacher-to-child ratios and provides a nurturing, innovative environment where tamariki are supported to thrive as confident learners and future leaders. The salary range for these roles is above Full Pay Parity. Find out more in our collective agreement. For more detailed information on the role, please refer to the position description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are passionate about te reo M\u0101ori, tikanga M\u0101ori, and kaupapa M\u0101ori education, whether through whakapapa, lived experience, professional experience, or a commitment to growing your connection to te ao M\u0101ori. You understand the importance of language, culture, and identity in supporting positive learning outcomes for tamariki. You bring warmth, humility, and positive energy to your work, build meaningful relationships with tamariki and wh\u0101nau, and enjoy being part of a collaborative, kaupapa-driven team. Most importantly, you share our commitment to nurturing mokopuna, strengthening te reo M\u0101ori, and empowering wh\u0101nau as active partners in their children's learning journey. E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking a Kaiako who: Champions the everyday use of te reo M\u0101ori and contributes to an environment where it is lived, nurtured, and celebrated. Has a sound understanding of te reo M\u0101ori me \u014dna tikanga and a commitment to continuing their language journey. Understands Te K\u014dhanga Reo kaupapa, Te Korowai, and Te Wh\u0101riki \u0101 Te K\u014dhanga Reo. Builds strong, authentic relationships with tamariki and wh\u0101nau. Values whanaungatanga, collaboration, and collective success. Demonstrates enthusiasm, professionalism, and initiative. Holds a recognised New Zealand ECE or primary teaching qualification and current teacher registration. Has experience as a qualified Kaiako and confidently applies relevant ECE theory and Te Wh\u0101riki in practice. Competency in te reo M\u0101ori me \u014dna tikanga is preferred. However, support and professional development are available for the right person who is committed to growing their capability. If you are passionate about creating a place where tamariki can learn, belong, thrive, and grow in their language and identity, we would love to hear from you. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer We are committed to supporting the wellbeing and professional growth of our kaimahi. Benefits include: Above Full Pay Parity remuneration Five weeks annual leave, including Christmas closure and Easter Tuesday Two paid professional development/teacher-only days each year Eligibility for both the University Te Reo M\u0101ori Allowance and the Ministry of Education Waha Rumaki Te Reo M\u0101ori Allowance Superannuation/KiwiSaver matching up to 6.75% Inclusive parental leave, discounted parking and childcare, career development opportunities, and access to a range of on-site staff benefits For more information, please visit our staff benefits page. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online by the 30th October 2026 and will be reviewed as they are received. Please include your CV and a cover letter outlining what you would bring to our kaupapa and how your experience and strengths align with this role. For a confidential conversation about the position, please contact Rahera Shepherd (Kaiwhakahaere) at rahera.shepherd@auckland.ac.nz . While we are happy to answer your questions, applications will only be accepted through the online application process. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland Located alongside Waipapa Marae at the University of Auckland, our teacher-led K\u014dhanga Reo -\u00a0the only k\u014dhanga reo on City Campus -\u00a0is dedicated to nurturing tamariki through a kaupapa M\u0101ori approach that honours te ao M\u0101ori, m\u0101tauranga M\u0101ori, and the aspirations of wh\u0101nau. Our long-standing team creates a warm, stable, and supportive environment grounded in whanaungatanga, manaakitanga, and a deep commitment to te reo M\u0101ori me \u014dna tikanga.", "department": "Campus Environment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155847348": {"title": "Te \u016anga Kairuruku, Schools and Community Recruitment Adviser", "description": "The opportunity | Te Whiwhinga mahi Te \u016anga Kairuruku, Schools and Community Recruitment Adviser , will be a key team member to implement recruitment initiatives for the school leaver market. You will be responsible for delivering mana-enhancing and inspiring activities for Years 13 secondary school students and maintaining a strong pipeline of opportunities for students to engage with Waipapa Taumata Rau. You will recruit new students into the university, through targeted events and will work with designated faculties in their portfolio of programmes as a relationship manager, ensuring that Faculties needs are being communicated and met by the Schools and Community Engagement function. This is a great opportunity for an experienced student adviser to specialise in a portfolio of programmes and industries. M\u014d t\u014d m\u0101tou r\u014dp\u016b | About the team Ko Waipapa Taumata Rau ko au, Ko au ko Waipapa Taumata Rau The p\u014dwhiri; a traditional tikanga based welcome for all. Bringing people together to share, engage and form a bond as one. The University of Auckland encapsulates this process as an essential model to welcome new prospective students and their wh\u0101nau to the University. The Schools and Community Engagement Office champion the values of Taumata Teitei, a Vision 2030 and a 2025 Strategic Plan for the University of Auckland.\u00a0 Their engagements will be mana enhancing, their k\u014drero inspiring and their actions empowering in preparing secondary students for their journey and the next phases of their voyage through education. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $73,400 to $99,400 per annum - Band F, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the position description .\u00a0 \n\n About you | He k\u014drero m\u014du Are you eager to take on an exciting opportunity? We are looking for a welcoming, motivated person with drive and desire to make a positive difference to the Schools and Community Engagement Team through a proven record of successful stakeholder engagement skills and an approach to work that embraces manakitanga, whanaungatanga, kaitiakitanga, flexibility and diversity.\u00a0 For a detailed description of the key tasks and responsibilities, please see the attached position description, however, to be successful in the role you will need to have the following attributes: A tertiary level qualification or equivalent experience Proven experience developing and maintaining relationships with key stakeholders. Experience of working with and understanding the needs of a diverse range of clients. Highly skilled communicator and presenter. Proficient computer literacy abilities and experience in delivering digital tools and resources. An understanding of the NZ secondary school system Have in-depth understanding of the tertiary environment especially in relation to the student experience. Demonstrable understanding, competence and confidence of te ao M\u0101ori Kia Whakamana i te Tangata - acknowledging, maintaining and restoring the mana and tapu of individuals, wh\u0101nau and communities. Agile experience, or an understanding of cross-function team ways of working in an agile environment. Ability to work with complex data Flexibility to support after hour events and ability to travel independently. \n\n Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare and a number of other discounts on internal and external services.\u00a0 For more information please visit\u00a0 Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 01 November 2026 \u00a0to be considered. Please include your cover letter and CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please reach out to Papalii Michael ( p.m.mauu@auckland.ac.nz ) if you have any questions about the role. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive and equitable environment. For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQITakat\u0101pui+, people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other equity issues go to\u00a0 www.equity.auckland.ac.nz . #LI-DNI Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, with a turnover of $1.3bn, including research revenue of over $300m generated across eight diverse faculties and two research institutes. We employ more than 5400 academic and professional staff to support over 45,000 students, making us one of NZ\u2019s largest employers. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.\u00a0 Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .", "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155657828": {"title": "Senior Brand Adviser \u2013 M\u0101ori", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Bring your marketing expertise, Te Ao M\u0101ori knowledge, and storytelling expertise to help shape how the University connects with M\u0101ori audiences. We\u2019re looking for an experienced Senior Brand Adviser \u2013 M\u0101ori to help shape how Waipapa Taumata Rau connects with M\u0101ori audiences. This is a senior marketing role for someone who knows their craft, understands what makes great marketing, and values connecting with their audience. You\u2019ll bring strong experience across brand, campaigns, audience engagement and activation, combined with genuine depth and confidence in Te Ao M\u0101ori. You\u2019ll use your marketing expertise and understanding of M\u0101ori audiences to shape ideas, campaigns and experiences that are relevant, meaningful and culturally grounded. You\u2019ll bring a strong understanding of what M\u0101ori audiences need, the ability to see the bigger picture and the confidence to turn insight into marketing that feels authentic and connects. Relationships will be central to the role. You\u2019ll build and maintain trusted connections with M\u0101ori organisations, communities and stakeholders, and be confident representing the University in M\u0101ori spaces, including hui and face-to-face engagement. You\u2019ll also bring an understanding of te reo M\u0101ori and a strong understanding of Te Ao M\u0101ori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi, providing cultural context and specialist advice across marketing, brand, content and audience engagement. You\u2019ll help strengthen how the University connects with tauira M\u0101ori, M\u0101ori staff and wider M\u0101ori communities, recognising the importance of belonging, identity and relationships across the University. The University\u2019s Kawea Ake strategy provides important strategic context for this work, supporting the integration of M\u0101ori knowledge, values and ways of working across the institution. Working across the University, you\u2019ll partner with colleagues in Marketing and Recruitment, the PVC M\u0101ori Office, faculties, Communications, the Kaiarataki deputy Pro-Vice-Chancellor M\u0101ori, and wider Strategic Engagement to strengthen how we connect with tauira M\u0101ori and M\u0101ori communities. What you\u2019ll be doing You\u2019ll help shape M\u0101ori-focused marketing and brand activity across the University, from campaigns and activations through to events and audience engagement. You will: Develop and deliver marketing activity that connects with M\u0101ori audiences. Bring M\u0101ori audience insight and Te Ao M\u0101ori knowledge into campaigns and brand activity. Shape ideas, messaging and experiences that are relevant, meaningful and culturally grounded. Build and maintain strong relationships with M\u0101ori communities and stakeholders. Provide specialist advice on M\u0101ori audiences, brand, culture and engagement. Bring cultural context and an understanding of te reo M\u0101ori to marketing and brand conversations. Work across teams to bring M\u0101ori campaigns, events and activations to life. Review M\u0101ori-focused content, ensuring it is culturally authentic and audience relevant. Identify opportunities to strengthen our M\u0101ori brand presence and engagement. Connect M\u0101ori audience insight, brand, recruitment and wider University priorities. Take ownership of your portfolio and confidently manage competing priorities. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent opportunity. The full salary range is $87,000 to $118,000 per annum, depending on skills and experience (Band G). We have a structured salary framework with three zones: developing (those developing into the role), fully effective (target), and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201\u2013$107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description .\u00a0 He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You\u2019re an experienced marketer who understands that meaningful engagement starts with knowing your audience, building relationships and earning trust. You recognise the importance of connecting authentically with M\u0101ori communities and bringing M\u0101ori perspectives into the way an organisation communicates and engages. You\u2019ll bring: Minimum 6 years\u2019 experience in marketing, brand, campaigns, audience engagement or a related field. Strong experience developing and delivering marketing campaigns, brand activity, activations, events and audience engagement initiatives. A strong understanding of M\u0101ori audiences and communities, with established relationships and connections with iwi and M\u0101ori organisations. Confidence engaging face-to-face with M\u0101ori communities and stakeholders, including attending hui and representing an organisation in M\u0101ori settings. Strong knowledge of Te Ao M\u0101ori and Te Tiriti o Waitangi, with confidence applying this knowledge in a professional marketing environment. An understanding of te reo M\u0101ori, including confidence with pronunciation and written usage, and the ability to bring cultural context and M\u0101ori perspectives into marketing advice. Strong relationship-building, influencing and communication skills, with the confidence to provide specialist advice and challenge thinking when needed. The ability to see the bigger picture, connect M\u0101ori audience insight with brand, recruitment and wider University priorities, and manage your portfolio confidently within a complex organisation. Experience in tertiary education, the public sector or another large and complex organisation would be valuable, but we\u2019re interested in the marketing expertise, M\u0101ori relationships, cultural knowledge and perspective you can bring. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you\u2019ll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world. Beyond the work itself, you\u2019ll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5-hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns. A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier. Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks\u2019 annual leave and an additional leave buy/sell scheme. Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including K\u014dhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks\u2019 full pay, and on-site parenting and feeding spaces. A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongo\u0101 services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, and discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo M\u0101ori learning programmes for staff. The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand\u2019s most diverse and complex organisations. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of 14th November 2026 . Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities, and relevant experience. Please reach out to Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations \u2013 We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Wh\u0101nau and support people \u2013 Should you be invited to an interview for this role, you are welcome to have a support person, or wh\u0101nau member accompany you. Please let us know if there is anything we can do to support you throughout the recruitment process. \u00a0 \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.\u00a0 Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .", "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155656589": {"title": "Weekend Receptionist - Student Accommodation ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Are you interested in weekend work and enjoy being the friendly face of a busy and vibrant community? We are seeking a Weekend Receptionist to join the team at Carlaw Park Student Village in Parnell. As part of the University's Accommodation team, you will play an important role in supporting one of New Zealand's largest student accommodation providers while helping create a positive experience for our residents and visitors. Based at the reception desk, you will be the primary point of contact for students, families, contractors and visitors on weekends. This is a highly visible, customer-focused role where no two days are the same. You will provide a warm welcome, respond to enquiries, support operational activities and help ensure the smooth day-to-day running of the residence. Home to almost 700 students, Carlaw Park Student Village is a diverse and active community. This role offers the opportunity to make a genuine difference to the student experience while developing your administration, customer service and problem-solving skills in a supportive environment. Your responsibilities will include: Acting as the first point of contact for residents, visitors and stakeholders, delivering a professional and welcoming customer experience Managing reception enquiries, phone calls, emails and resident requests Supporting accommodation administration, including key management, mail handling and payment enquiries Coordinating with contractors, cleaners and service providers to support operational requirements Assisting the Resident Manager with day-to-day property operations and facilities-related activities Supporting maintenance reporting, facility inspections and ensuring high presentation standards across the residence This is a part-time (15 hours per week), permanent position . The salary range for this role is $56,550 to $67,000 (pro rata) , negotiable depending on skills and experience. The usual hours of work are Saturday and Sunday, 8:30am to 4:30pm , inclusive of an unpaid lunch break. A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are an experienced administrator or receptionist who enjoys working with people and providing exceptional customer service. You are confident communicating with a diverse range of stakeholders and are comfortable balancing multiple priorities while maintaining a professional and friendly approach. Weekend work fits in with your lifestyle, and you thrive in a busy environment where initiative, organisation and attention to detail are important. Whether you're supporting students, coordinating contractors or assisting with operational tasks, you bring a solutions-focused mindset and enjoy contributing to a positive team culture. Most importantly, you are passionate about creating a welcoming and supportive environment where residents feel valued and supported throughout their accommodation experience. \u00a0 E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking Proven administration, reception or customer service experience within a busy environment Strong organisational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to manage multiple priorities Excellent written and verbal communication skills Strong interpersonal skills and a genuine commitment to customer service Confidence using Microsoft Office applications and learning new systems A current First Aid Certificate would be advantageous, but is not essential Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on\u2011site amenities Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Wednesday, 21 October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Karyn Baker, Resident Manager at karyn.baker@auckland.ac.nz \u00a0#LI-DNI \n\n \u00a0 \u00a0 Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz", "department": "Campus Environment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155605879": {"title": "Lifeguard - Full Time ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking a permanent \u00a0Lifeguard to join our team at Hiwa, the university\u2019s state-of-the-art Recreation Centre located right in the heart of Auckland on Symonds Street. This is much more than a typical pool role as you will be working at a vibrant hub for sport and wellbeing that serves as the social heartbeat for our students and the wider community. Our Aquatic Hall is a world-class facility featuring a versatile stainless-steel pool, dive wells with springboards, and premium recovery spaces like our spa and sauna. As a lifeguard in this engaging environment, you will be responsible for the safety of a diverse range of patrons while enjoying the buzz of a central city campus. If you have a background with the university or simply thrive in an active and social setting, this is a brilliant chance to develop your professional skills in a facility that represents the very best in modern recreation design. The main responsibilities of this role include: General lifeguard duties, including pool supervision Providing exceptional customer service Pool water testing and monitoring Ensuring pool safety and H&S standards are met This is a permanent, full-time position\u00a0 ( 37.5 hours per week) . The salary range is $55,634 to $60,000 per annum ($28.53 to $30.70 per hour). The final offer will be negotiable depending on your skills and experience. The set shifts for this role are: Tuesday to Thursday: 5:30am - 1:30pm Friday: 1:15pm - 9:30pm Saturday: 10:30am - 6:30pm or\u00a0 Sunday: 10:30am - 6:30pm Monday: 5:30am - 1:30pm Tuesday - Thursday: 1:30pm - 9:30pm A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked\u00a0 HERE . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About You Our ideal candidate is not just a confident swimmer, but a proactive, responsible, and approachable professional. We are looking for individuals with a genuine interest for public safety, strong customer service skills, and a dedication to creating a safe, positive, and genuinely inclusive environment for all university community members. This is a dynamic, high-visibility role where your self-awareness and attention to detail are key to protecting our patrons. Training & Qualifications: While holding a current lifeguard qualification is highly desirable, it is not essential we provide full, comprehensive training for the right candidate. A Pool Lifeguard Practising Certificate and/or a First Aid Certificate would be a definite bonus and highly advantageous. E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking: Experience working as a lifeguard is ideal, or demonstrable success in any other fast-paced, customer-focused role. Outstanding customer service and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage positively with a diverse range of people. Strong communication skills with an eye for detail regarding health and safety procedures and compliance. Highly confident swimming skills and the physical fitness required for effective pool supervision and rescue. The ability to be fully reliable, punctual, and available for the rostered shifts. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver , matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance , memberships , and on\u2011site amenities Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday 18th October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications. For a confidential discussion, you are welcome to contact Emily Spurgeon, Aquatic Team Leader at emily.spurgeon@auckland.ac.nz \n\n \u00a0 \u00a0 Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz", "department": "Campus Life"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155380458": {"title": "Lecturer in Analytical Chemistry - Te Kura M\u0101tai Mat\u016b |The School of Chemical Sciences", "description": "M\u014d t\u014d m\u0101tou r\u014dp\u016b | About the team The Analytical Chemistry programme is hosted in Te Kura M\u0101tai Mat\u016b |The School of Chemical Sciences , the largest chemistry-related department in New Zealand, with over 30 permanent academic staff and over 150 PhD students. The School has a very strong international reputation in research, with collaborations spanning the globe, and it has a high-quality research infrastructure. As well as our undergraduate and postgraduate programmes in Analytical Chemistry \u2013 including Forensic Science and Environmental Science \u2013 we also offer postgraduate programmes in Food Science, Green Chemical Science, Medicinal Chemistry and Wine Science that could provide collaborative research opportunities for successful candidates. The School is committed to teaching excellence and to\u00a0building connections with our community. We are striving to reflect the demographics of our student base and Aotearoa. Our staff and students can connect with our Rainbow and Women in Science networks, Parenting support, our Waipapa Marae , and our flagship Tu\u0101kana M\u0101ori and Pacific support programme. More information about the School and the researchers in our School can be found on our webpage: https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/science/about-the-faculty/school-of-chemical-sciences.html Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity The School of Chemical Sciences seeks to appoint a permanent academic faculty member at the Lecturer level in Analytical Chemistry. The appointment will be within the broad area of analytical chemistry. Candidates will be considered on their merits, with the primary criteria for selection detailed below. Preference will be given to candidates who can also contribute to teaching and research in the applied disciplines in the School, including forensic and environmental chemistry, and have a clear vision of how their research plan will be implemented in a New Zealand context. This is a full-time (40 hours per week) permanent position. The appointees will be required to contribute to our offshore teaching commitments, including travel to other countries, as part of the role. The remuneration is between (L1) $99,788 \u2013 (L7) $119,204 per annum, dependent on skills and experience\u00a0 For more detailed information, please refer to the Position Description . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you Selection Criteria : The successful candidate will be selected based on demonstrating the following criteria: you have the potential to become a world-class researcher in your respective field of analytical chemistry with purposeful and impactful research of relevance in the real world. you have the potential to implement modern methods relating to analytical chemistry, with an independent research programme funded by external grants, and strong research collaborations with other institutions and industry. you have the potential to become a teacher and supervisor who reflects the latest advances in analytical chemistry and your field with a commitment to innovative teaching, including navigating the use of AI as a teaching and research tool, supervision excellence, and contributing to research-informed teaching and supervision within the broad area of analytical chemistry. you are interested and are active in dimensions beyond research and teaching; for example, mentoring and advising students and colleagues, public outreach, engaging with industry, and science communication.\u00a0 you are collaborative and collegial \u2013 willing to support the successful operation of the analytical chemistry and forensic science programmes within the School. you have a high-level understanding of and demonstrated commitment to the principles of inclusion, diversity, equity, and access in a university context. We are looking for an applicant with a conferred PhD in Analytical Chemistry, Forensic Chemistry, Environmental Chemistry or a closely related discipline. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University is in the heart of Auckland/T\u0101maki Makaurau, a harbour-side city with rich cultural diversity, beaches, islands, iconic volcanoes, and lush forests. Auckland is consistently ranked in the top tier in global quality of life surveys. \u00a0 The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through: Flexible employment practices (including working from home, flexible hours) Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with five weeks annual leave\u00a0 In addition, we also offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, relocation support if required, and other discounts on internal and external services.\u00a0 For more information, please visit\u00a0 Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 13th November 2026 to be considered. To apply for this role, please upload the following four documents: A brief cover letter explaining your interest in the position explicitly addressing each of the selection criteria above (1-2 pages). A current curriculum vitae (CV) which includes the names and contact details of at least three referees (preferably including a current or previous supervisor/manager).\u00a0Referees will not be contacted without your prior approval. A separate statement which includes your research vision for the next 5 years, including the potential for collaborations within the School, the wider University of Auckland and Industry, and plans to obtain research funding support (1-2 pages). A separate statement which includes your philosophy on teaching, supervision, and student support (1-2 pages). Please look at the School\u2019s webpage ( https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/science/about-the-faculty/school-of-chemical-sciences.html ) for further information, and contact the Head of School, Prof Duncan McGillivray ( d.mcgillivray@auckland.ac.nz ) or Prof SallyAnn Harbison ( sallyann.harbison@auckland.ac.nz ) if you have other questions. Please note that we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science\u00a0 at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tu\u0101kana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, M\u0101ori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae .", "department": "Faculty of Science"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155369940": {"title": "Property Manager - Commercial & Retail", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity This is an opportunity to take ownership of the University\u2019s commercial and retail lease portfolio, leading the governance, administration and optimisation of a diverse range of leased properties, retail tenancies and commercial occupancies. Reporting to the Associate Director - Commercial, you will play a key role in maximising portfolio performance, supporting strategic objectives, and ensuring a positive campus experience for students, staff and visitors. Some key responsibilities include: Lead the management of the University\u2019s commercial and retail lease portfolio, including leases, licences and occupancy agreements. Build and maintain effective relationships with landlords, tenants, operators, property managers, consultants and internal stakeholders. Manage lease negotiations, renewals, variations and commercial agreements, ensuring favourable outcomes and alignment with University objectives. Monitor portfolio performance, maintain accurate lease data, oversee critical lease events and provide commercial reporting and recommendations. Identify opportunities to improve commercial value, optimise portfolio performance and support long-term strategic planning. Ensure compliance with contractual, legislative, policy and insurance requirements across the portfolio. Work collaboratively across Campus Environment, Finance, Risk, Legal and operational teams to deliver effective lease governance and service outcomes. This is a permanent, full-time position (37.5 hours per week). The salary range is $116,000 to $125,000 per annum depending on your skills and experience. A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE . He k\u014drero m\u014du | About You You are an experienced property professional with strong commercial acumen and a practical, solutions-focused approach. Comfortable operating in a complex stakeholder environment, you bring sound judgement, strong relationship management skills and the ability to balance operational delivery with strategic thinking. Demonstrated experience managing commercial lease portfolios, lease negotiations and contract administration. Proven ability to build productive relationships and influence a broad range of internal and external stakeholders. Strong analytical capability, with experience interpreting portfolio performance data and preparing commercial reports and recommendations. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the professionalism to engage confidently across all levels of an organisation. Highly organised, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines and maintain a high level of accuracy. A proactive and self-directed working style, with a track record of exercising sound judgement and taking ownership of outcomes. Strong customer focus and a collaborative approach to service delivery, stakeholder engagement and problem solving. Relevant tertiary qualifications in property, law, business or a related discipline, or equivalent professional experience. Desirable experience includes working within a large or complex organisation, knowledge of the New Zealand property environment, and experience identifying and implementing process improvements that enhance efficiency and service delivery. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 9,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 50,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver, matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance, memberships, and on\u2011site amenities \n\n Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday 1st November, 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV. Please note that we do not accept email applications. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz", "department": "Campus Environment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155160289": {"title": "Executive Assistant", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity As Executive Assistant to the \u00a0Director - Education Strategy & Enablement and\u00a0Pro Vice-Chancellor Student Success and Experience , you\u2019ll provide high\u2011quality, confidential executive support that keeps a complex portfolio moving, freeing the DVC\u2019s time to focus on strategy, leadership, and outcomes for learners and educators. You\u2019ll run the day\u2011to\u2011day rhythm: prioritising a dynamic calendar, guiding communications, coordinating key meetings and committees, and ensuring decisions and actions flow smoothly across the University. You\u2019ll also own critical admin processes (travel, expenses, documentation) and be a trusted first point of contact for senior stakeholders. Key responsibilities include: Executive support: Advanced diary/inbox management, meeting and committee coordination, minute\u2011taking, travel and expenses, and preparation of briefs, presentations, and correspondence. Operations & workflow: Maintain efficient systems and procedures, manage filing and document control, and support portfolio projects and events to agreed timeframes and quality. Stakeholder liaison: Build strong relationships with internal and external partners; act with discretion, judgement, and professionalism when handling sensitive matters. Customer service & improvement: Deliver reliable, accurate service; seek feedback; and contribute to continuous improvement across the portfolio\u2019s processes. On\u2011campus support as needed for key meetings, forums, and visiting delegations. The role is a full-time,\u202fpermanent role (37.5 hours per week) The remuneration is $82,000-$94,000+ per annum, dependent on skills and experience. For more information about this role, please refer to the Position Description .\u00a0 He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you We\u2019re seeking confident and experienced Executive Assistants who excel in a busy and complex environment and take pride in delivering effective support. To succeed in this role, you\u2019ll bring: Experience (5+ years) supporting leaders as either a Personal Assistant, Executive Assistant or with senior administrative experience. Exceptional organisational and time management skills, with the ability to anticipate needs, manage multiple priorities, and stay calm under pressure. Outstanding communication skills \u2013 written, verbal, and interpersonal High-level proficiency in Microsoft 365 (Outlook, Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams) and a natural ability for learning new systems and tools, including AI Proactive, solutions-focused approach, demonstrating sound judgement, discretion, and professionalism in handling sensitive and confidential information. What we offer | Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou We offer you more than just a job. We offer you the opportunity to be part of a dynamic, world-class educational institution that provides benefits and support throughout your career to help you develop and grow. We are committed to providing a supportive working environment through: Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme A competitive salary with\u00a0 five weeks of annual leave . With the option to buy and sell additional leave. Flexible employment practices . Including partial working from home and adaptive working hours. A generous and inclusive\u00a0 parental leave \u00a0policy. In addition, we offer career development programmes, discounted car parking, on-site childcare options, leisure and recreation facilities, and a number of other discounted products and services.\u00a0 For more information, please visit\u00a0 Staff Benefits . \n\n Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 25th October, 2026 \u00a0to be considered. Please include your cover letter and/or CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above and in the position description.\u00a0 Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email.\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau \u00a0 | The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is Aotearoa\u2019s leading university and largest research institution, ranked in the Global top 100.\u00a0 We employ over 9,000 staff and support 50,000 students to achieve their ambitions. As one of New Zealand\u2019s most enduring brands, the University leads the way in teaching and research excellence positively impacting the lives of its people, communities and society at large.", "department": "Deputy VC (Education)"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000155127879": {"title": "Genomics Technologist", "description": "Te\u00a0Whiwhinga\u00a0mahi | The opportunity\u00a0 We are seeking a\u00a0Genomics Technologist\u00a0to join\u00a0the\u00a0Grafton Clinical Genomics (GCG)\u00a0team within the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences.\u00a0GCG is\u00a0an\u202fIANZ accredited laboratory\u202flocated in the Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences, University of Auckland.\u00a0GCG\u00a0delivers\u00a0high-quality genomics services that support both clinical diagnostics and research initiatives.\u00a0 This is a hands-on laboratory role where you will provide day-to-day technical expertise and contribute to the\u00a0delivery of a high-quality genomics service.\u00a0You will utilise advanced research\u00a0and clinical technologies including MALDI-TOF Mass Spectrometry, NanoString nCounter, Illumina and MGI Next Generation Sequencing platforms, while supporting researchers, students and clinical stakeholders.\u00a0 You will also\u00a0have the opportunity to\u00a0further develop your genomics expertise as part of an expanding team of research technicians, professional staff and students.\u00a0 Key\u00a0responsibilities:\u00a0 Operate and maintain specialised genomics equipment including MALDI-TOF Mass Spectrometry, NanoString, Illumina, MGI NGS and Real-Time PCR platforms.\u00a0\u00a0 Process and analyse clinical and research samples in accordance with laboratory procedures and quality standards.\u00a0\u00a0 Maintain laboratory protocols, documentation and records to support IANZ (ISO 15189) accreditation requirements.\u00a0\u00a0 Provide technical training, advice and support to researchers, students and laboratory users.\u00a0\u00a0 Assist with equipment maintenance, calibration and ongoing facility operations.\u00a0\u00a0 Support laboratory compliance, health and safety, and regulatory requirements.\u00a0\u00a0 Contribute to the development and implementation of new methods and workflows.\u00a0 This is a\u00a0 full time \u00a0opportunity for a\u00a0 fixed term \u00a0of 27 months\u00a0 The salary range for this\u00a0role is\u00a0 $73,400 -\u00a0$99,400 \u00a0depending on skills and experience\u00a0 He k\u014drero\u00a0m\u014du\u00a0|\u202fAbout you\u00a0 You will be a technically capable laboratory professional with experience working in molecular genetics, genomics or a related discipline. You'll enjoy working in a collaborative environment, take pride in producing accurate, high-quality work, and be comfortable balancing hands-on laboratory responsibilities with supporting others.\u00a0 You'll bring a methodical approach, strong attention to detail, and the ability to work both independently and as part of a team. A willingness to share knowledge and train others will help you succeed in this role.\u00a0 Our ideal candidate will\u00a0bring:\u00a0 A Bachelor of Science in Molecular Genetics, Medical Laboratory Science or a related field.\u00a0\u00a0 Preferably a New Zealand registered Medical Laboratory Scientist or Technician in Molecular Genetics with a current practising certificate.\u00a0 Experience working with clinical samples and within an ISO 15189 accredited laboratory (or similar quality environment)\u00a0 At least 2-3 years' post-graduate experience in molecular genetics, genomics or a similar laboratory setting.\u00a0 Experience operating advanced genomics instrumentation and working with DNA and RNA workflows.\u00a0 Strong attention to detail, good communication skills, and the ability to support and train laboratory users.\u00a0\u00a0 Ng\u0101\u00a0\u0101huatanga\u00a0kei\u00a0a\u00a0m\u0101tou\u00a0| What we offer\u00a0 The University of Auckland is committed to providing an excellent working environment through:\u00a0 Flexible employment practices and a culture that supports work-life balance\u00a0 Career development programmes\u00a0 A competitive salary with five weeks\u2019 annual leave\u00a0 6.75% superannuation scheme\u00a0 For more information, please visit\u202f Staff Benefits .\u00a0 Me\u00a0p\u0113hea\u00a0te\u00a0tuku\u00a0tono |\u202fHow to apply\u202f\u00a0 Applications should be submitted online, before the closing date of\u202f 27\u00a0October 2026 \u202fand include an up-to-date CV and cover letter outlining what strengths you will bring to this role.\u00a0 If you have\u00a0any\u00a0questions, please email\u00a0Jason Copedo\u00a0via\u00a0j.copedo@auckland.ac.nz\u00a0 Please note we are happy to answer your\u00a0questions,\u00a0but we do not accept applications via email \u00a0 \u00a0 #LI-DNI \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \n\n Waipapa\u00a0Taumata\u00a0Rau\u202f| The University of Auckland\u00a0 The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties, two large-scale research institutes and\u00a0a number of\u00a0professional service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence.\u00a0 Our lecturers and health researchers are regarded as experts in their fields, frequently directing global research projects designed to better the lives of New Zealanders and the world, and in turn passing this knowledge on to our students.", "department": "Faculty of Medical and Health Sciences"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154912798": {"title": "Senior Technician - Plant Molecular Biology - Ko te whare P\u016btaiao | The Faculty of Science ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Help power the science behind tomorrow's discoveries We're looking for a Senior Technician \u2013 Plant Molecular Biology to join our Technical Services team. This is an opportunity to provide specialist technical support across plant science teaching and research, working with academics, researchers, students, and laboratory users. You'll play an important role in keeping teaching laboratories, research facilities, equipment, and biological resources operating safely and effectively. From supporting practical teaching sessions and maintaining specialist equipment to coordinating technical resources and supporting plant molecular biology research, you'll help create the environment researchers and students need to do their best work. What you'll be doing Providing specialist technical support across plant cellular and molecular biology, biochemistry and genetics Supporting the preparation and delivery of practical teaching sessions, including testing experiments, equipment and protocols Coordinating laboratory resources, biological materials, equipment and facilities to support teaching and research Developing, documenting and improving laboratory methods, protocols and processes Maintaining specialist equipment and ensuring laboratories are safe, compliant and ready for use Training, coaching and supporting students, Teaching Assistants, researchers and laboratory users Supporting health, safety, containment and regulatory requirements across assigned laboratories and facilities This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. The salary range is $73,400 to $86,403 per annum, depending on skills and experience. For more detailed information, please refer to the\u00a0 Position Description He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You'll bring a strong background in plant cellular and molecular biology, along with a practical, solutions-focused approach. You'll be comfortable working across teaching and research environments, troubleshooting technical issues and managing competing priorities while maintaining high standards of accuracy, safety and organisation. We're particularly interested in hearing from candidates who have: A postgraduate degree in Biological Sciences, Plant Science or a related discipline 3+ years' experience in plant cellular and molecular biology post-BSc Experience explaining and resolving difficult technical issues Strong accuracy, attention to detail, and organisational skills The ability to multitask, prioritise and manage your workload effectively Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively Experience mentoring or instructing others in a laboratory environment would be advantageous Previous experience in a tertiary environment would be advantageous Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer The University of Auckland is New Zealand's leading University and maintains significant computational, laboratory, and analytic facilities. Auckland is frequently rated as one of the world's most liveable cities. The University is committed to providing an excellent working environment through the following: \u00b7 Flexible employment practices (including working from home and flexible hours) \u00b7 Up to 6.75% company superannuation scheme \u00b7 A competitive salary with five weeks' annual leave In addition, we also offer career development programs, discounted car parking, a generous parental leave allowance, childcare, and other discounts on internal and external services. For more information, please visit Staff Benefits . Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Applications must be submitted online, by the closing date of 27th October\u00a02026 to be considered. \u00a0Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experience detailed above. Please reach out to Wendy Payne (Technical Team Leader) via w.payne@auckland.ac.nz for a confidential conversation. Please note we are happy to answer your questions, but we do not accept applications by email. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland Ko te whare P\u016btaiao\u00a0|\u00a0The Faculty of Science\u00a0 at the University of Auckland is the largest and most highly ranked science faculty in New Zealand. The Faculty of Science is ranked among the top 90 science faculties worldwide by the QS World University Rankings. The 10 departments and schools that form the Faculty of Science represent the breadth and diversity of science in our modern world. Our staff and students can connect with our flagship groups including Tu\u0101kana , Rainbow, Women of Colour, M\u0101ori, Pacific, Muslim, and Disabled Staff network and our Waipapa Marae .", "department": "Faculty of Science"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154892898": {"title": "Senior Brand Adviser - Pacific ", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity Bring your marketing expertise, Pacific audience insight and creativity to a role where you can shape how the University connects with Pacific communities. We\u2019re looking for an experienced Senior Brand Adviser \u2013 Pacific to help shape how Waipapa Taumata Rau connects with Pacific audiences. This is a marketing role for someone who knows their craft and understands what makes great marketing work. You\u2019ll bring strong experience across brand, campaigns, audience engagement and activation, combined with a genuine understanding of Pacific communities and culture. You\u2019ll use your marketing expertise and Pacific insight to shape ideas, campaigns and experiences that are relevant, engaging and culturally grounded. You\u2019ll be someone who can look at the bigger picture, understand what our audiences need and turn that insight into marketing that connects. Relationship building will be an important part of the role. You\u2019ll develop and maintain strong connections with Pacific communities, organisations, partners and stakeholders, using those relationships to strengthen our understanding and engagement. You\u2019ll work across the University, partnering with colleagues in Marketing and Recruitment, Communications and Engagement and the Office of the Pro Vice-Chancellor Pacific. We\u2019re looking for someone who is confident working independently, comfortable providing specialist advice and able to influence and collaborate across a large and complex organisation. What you\u2019ll be doing You\u2019ll help shape Pacific-focused marketing and brand activity across the University, from campaigns and activations through to events and audience engagement. Develop and deliver marketing activity that connects with Pacific audiences. Bring Pacific insight into campaigns, messaging and experiences. Shape ideas and activations that are relevant, engaging and culturally authentic. Build strong relationships with Pacific communities, organisations and stakeholders. Provide specialist advice on Pacific audiences, marketing and brand. Work across teams to bring campaigns, events and activations to life. Review Pacific-focused content, ensuring it is relevant and culturally responsive. Identify opportunities to strengthen our Pacific brand presence and engagement. Connect audience insight, brand, recruitment and wider University priorities. Take ownership of your portfolio and confidently manage competing priorities, contributing to Ala o le Moana Pacific Strategy. - https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/about-us/about-the-university/the-university/official-publications/ala-o-le-moana.html Support the University's Pacific student and community aspirations Engage Pacific students, staff, alumni and communities to inform and co-design marketing approaches, campaigns and experiences. Strengthen the University\u2019s Pacific brand and reputation through authentic representation of Pacific people, communities, cultures, languages and stories. This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent opportunity. The full salary range is $87,000 to $118,000 per annum, depending on skills and experience (Band G). We have a structured salary framework with three zones: developing (those developing into the role), fully effective (target), and contributing beyond what is expected of the role (upper). Appointments are typically made within the target range of $93,201 \u2013 $107,500 per annum, depending on skills, experience, and internal equity. We welcome conversations about your expectations and what matters to you in your overall package. Any offer will be made within the advertised range and guided by fairness, consistency and our commitment to pay equity . For more detailed information about the role and requirements, please refer to the Position Description. \u00a0 He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You\u2019re an experienced marketer who knows that great marketing starts with really understanding your audience. You\u2019ll bring strong marketing and brand experience, along with a genuine understanding of Pacific communities and culture. You\u2019ll be confident using your expertise to shape campaigns, ideas and experiences that feel relevant and authentic, and comfortable building relationships and bringing people together to make things happen. You\u2019ll bring: minimum 6 years\u2019 experience in marketing, brand, campaigns or audience engagement. Strong experience developing and delivering marketing campaigns, activations and events. A strong understanding of Pacific audiences, communities and culture. Experience building and maintaining genuine relationships with Pacific communities, organisations and stakeholders. The ability to turn audience insight into strong ideas, messaging and experiences. Strong knowledge of brand, marketing strategy, audience insight and activation. Confidence providing specialist marketing advice and influencing stakeholders. Strong cultural judgement and a commitment to authentic Pacific representation. The ability to see the bigger picture and connect the dots. Strong communication, relationship-building and influencing skills. The ability to work autonomously, manage priorities and take ownership. Demonstrated ability to work within Pacific cultural protocols and ways of engaging, including an understanding of reciprocal relationship-building. We\u2019d particularly value someone who already has strong connections across Pacific communities and organisations, and who is confident building relationships face-to-face and engaging in community settings. Pacific language capability, including confidence with pronunciation and written usage, would also be welcomed. You\u2019ll be part of a team that is committed to creating stronger connections with Pacific communities and supporting Pacific student aspirations and participation at the University. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Our scale creates opportunities that few organisations can offer. From research and innovation to education, reputation and community engagement, you\u2019ll work across a diverse range of communications challenges while contributing to initiatives that have real and lasting impact for staff, students, our communities and the world. Beyond the work itself, you\u2019ll be joining a vibrant and inclusive community. From staff networks and cultural communities to social clubs, volunteering opportunities, wellbeing initiatives and campus events, there are countless ways to connect, contribute and build meaningful relationships across the University. We also offer: A commitment to work/life balance with a 37.5-hour working week, flexible hours and hybrid/remote working patterns. A competitive remuneration package with up to 6.75% super, annual salary increase with performance-based multiplier. Time to recharge and disconnect with 5 weeks\u2019 annual leave and an additional leave buy/sell scheme. Support for parents and carers with subsidised on-site childcare (including K\u014dhanga Reo), gender-neutral parental leave up to 9 weeks\u2019 full pay, and on-site parenting and feeding spaces. A commitment to the wellbeing of our people with confidential access to a range of EAP services, including mental health counselling, financial guidance, neurodiversity coaching and rongo\u0101 services, a world-class on-site fitness centre, access to student specialists across our supervised training clinics, and discounted health insurance. The opportunity to grow with access to an extensive programme of professional, leadership and cultural development, including digital capability coaching, specialised leadership development programmes, and Te Ao and Te Reo M\u0101ori learning programmes for staff. The opportunity to build your career with one of New Zealand\u2019s most diverse and complex organisations. Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit an application online by the closing date of 11th November 2026 . Please include your cover letter and CV, highlighting your interest in the role, your capabilities, and relevant experience. Please reach out to Sharon Kishore (Recruitment Consultant) via sharon.kishore@auckland.ac.nz with any questions. Reasonable accommodations \u2013 We are committed to building a more equitable and inclusive workforce and workplace, so we encourage applicants to reach out to discuss any reasonable accommodations throughout the recruitment process, including alternative ways to submit applications. Wh\u0101nau and support people \u2013 Should you be invited to an interview for this role, you are welcome to have a support person, or wh\u0101nau member accompany you. Please let us know if there is anything we can do to support you throughout the recruitment process. \n\n Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau\u00a0| The University of Auckland The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading University. With over 6,000 academic and professional staff supporting over 46,000 students, we are one of NZ\u2019s largest employers.\u00a0 We have six academic faculties and 13 service divisions creating a diverse, dynamic and complex organisational culture that demands excellence. The University of Auckland creates globally transformative impacts through our distinctive strengths in world-leading research, scholarship, teaching, and collaborative partnerships, inspired by our unique position in Aotearoa New Zealand and the Asia-Pacific.\u00a0 Our mission and values place a strong emphasis on our student body and scholarly community, and we work with both the community and enterprise to advance the well-being of the people of Auckland and New Zealand, with a clear commitment to sustainability .", "department": "Marketing and Recruitment"}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154891929": {"title": "Summer Assistants | Kai\u0101whina Raumati", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity We are seeking Summer Assistants | Kai\u0101whina Raumati to join our Accommodation team over the summer period. This is a unique opportunity to live and work within our residential communities , helping to create a safe, welcoming and enjoyable experience for both students remaining in accommodation and the many guests who stay with us over summer. Working closely with our Accommodation Management teams, you will provide peer support, customer service and operational assistance while helping to foster an inclusive and supportive residential environment. During the summer months, our residences host a variety of guests, including university students attending summer programmes, conference delegates, intern groups and other visitors. You will play an important role in ensuring their experience is positive, safe and memorable. This is a live-in role and an excellent opportunity for students or recent graduates looking to develop leadership, customer service and community-building skills. You'll gain hands-on experience supporting people from diverse backgrounds while contributing to a vibrant residential community. Your responsibilities will include: Providing friendly, professional support and guidance to residents and summer guests, acting as a key point of contact within the accommodation community Supporting front desk operations, guest arrivals and departures, enquiries, administration and accommodation services Helping to foster an inclusive, respectful and supportive residential environment through positive engagement and peer support Assisting with events, activities and community-building initiatives that enhance the residential experience Responding appropriately to incidents and wellbeing concerns, escalating matters to Accommodation Management when required Supporting health, safety and emergency procedures while helping maintain safe and welcoming accommodation environments A full description of your duties and the role are provided in the position description linked HERE What you need to know These are fixed-term (November 2026 to February 2027) , part-time positions working 15 hours per week at $29.00 per hour . Contracts will run from either the 16 November 2026 to 18 February 2027 or 23 November 2026 to 18 February 2027 Summer Assistants will live onsite and work a roster that can include evenings, weekends, and the Christmas and New Year period. Note: A Police Vetting check will be required and arranged for by UoA before you can start. He k\u014drero m\u014du | About you You are approachable, reliable and enjoy helping others. You understand the importance of creating inclusive communities and are comfortable supporting people from a wide range of backgrounds and experiences. Whether welcoming guests, supporting residents or responding to day-to-day challenges, you bring a positive and service-focused approach. Ideally, you are a current University of Auckland student who plans to remain in Auckland over the summer period and is either intending to stay in accommodation or is happy to live in the residence assigned to you. You are comfortable working a roster that includes evenings, weekends and some shifts across the festive season, including Christmas and New Year. You are organised, able to work independently and comfortable balancing multiple responsibilities. You enjoy being part of a team, communicating with others and finding practical solutions when issues arise. Most importantly, you are committed to creating a safe, respectful and welcoming environment where everyone can have a positive residential experience. E rapua ana m\u0101tou | We are seeking Completion of at least one year of tertiary study, and ideally (not essential) a current UoA student An interest in supporting students and contributing to a positive residential community Strong customer service and interpersonal skills, with the ability to engage with people from diverse backgrounds Good organisational skills and the ability to work independently and manage competing priorities Sound problem-solving skills and the confidence to respond appropriately to a range of situations Basic confidence using Microsoft Office applications, with a full New Zealand driver's licence considered advantageous Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Waipapa Taumata Rau | The University of Auckland is New Zealand\u2019s leading university, guided by a commitment to excellence and service to our communities. With more than 6,000 academic and professional staff, we support a diverse community of over 47,000 students. Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: An opportunity to gain valuable work experience Competitive pay of $29.00 per hour Flexible 15-hour work week Opportunity to live on campus, with accommodation available at competitive rates Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply Please submit your application online by the closing date of Sunday, \u00a0 25\u00a0October 2026 . Your application should include: A brief Cover Letter explaining why you're interested in this role and how your skills match the requirements. Your updated CV . Please note that we do not accept email applications \n\n \u00a0 \u00a0 Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz", "department": ""}, "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/744000154889649": {"title": "Finance Business Partner - Research", "description": "Te Whiwhinga mahi | The opportunity 12-Month Fixed-Term Opportunity This is a strategic Finance Business Partner role supporting the Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research portfolio. Working closely with senior leaders, you will provide trusted financial advice, planning, and performance insights that support informed decision-making and help deliver the University's research and organisational objectives. You will play a key role in strengthening financial capability, driving continuous improvement, and ensuring financial sustainability across a complex and dynamic environment. Partner with senior academic and professional leaders to develop financial strategies, annual plans, budgets, and forecasts aligned to organisational priorities. Provide high-quality financial analysis, reporting, and decision support to enable effective planning, performance monitoring, and resource allocation. Act as a trusted adviser to leadership teams, translating financial information into practical insights and recommendations that support strategic outcomes. Lead and contribute to initiatives that improve financial performance, operational effectiveness, and organisational capability across the portfolio. Develop strong relationships across faculties, research institutes, service divisions, and the wider finance community to ensure a collaborative and consistent approach to business partnering. Support financial governance and compliance by ensuring appropriate controls, policies, and performance frameworks are in place and effectively applied. Provide leadership and mentoring to finance team members while contributing to a culture of continuous improvement, innovation, and service excellence. This is a 12-month secondment/fixed-term, full-time role (37.5 hours per week, Monday to Friday). The salary range for this role is $118,000-$136,000 depending on skills and experience.\u00a0 For more information, please see\u00a0 Position Description He k\u014drero m\u014du | About You You are a commercially astute finance professional who enjoys building strong relationships and influencing positive outcomes. Comfortable working in a complex organisation, you bring a collaborative approach, strong analytical capability, and the confidence to engage with senior stakeholders on both strategic and operational matters. A tertiary qualification in accounting, commerce, or a related discipline, together with Chartered Accountant status (CA ANZ, CPA, or equivalent). Significant experience in finance business partnering, financial planning, budgeting, and performance management within a large and complex organisation. Demonstrated ability to build credibility with senior leaders and provide clear, evidence-based advice that supports sound decision-making. Strong relationship management, communication, influencing, and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work across diverse groups and priorities. Experience leading or contributing to change initiatives, continuous improvement programmes, and the development of innovative solutions to business challenges. Proven ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery, while managing competing priorities and delivering high-quality outcomes. A collaborative leadership style with experience mentoring, supporting, or developing team members to achieve shared goals. Strong digital literacy, including an interest in emerging technologies and the ability to apply sound judgement when evaluating and using new tools. Ng\u0101 \u0101huatanga kei a m\u0101tou | What we offer Guided by our values , we work collaboratively to advance knowledge, foster equity and inclusion, and contribute meaningfully to New Zealand\u2019s social, cultural, and economic well-being. We cultivate an environment that supports your growth and rewards excellence with a range of staff benefits , including: Competitive salary and five weeks of annual leave Discounted public Transport for permanent and long-term fixed term staff Superannuation/KiwiSaver, matching up to 6.75% Flexible working arrangements, including working from home and flexible hours Leading, and inclusive parental leave provisions Career development opportunities and a range of benefits, including discounted insurance, memberships, and on\u2011site amenities \u00a0 \n\n Me p\u0113hea te tuku tono | How to apply \nApplications must be submitted online, by the closing date of Sunday 18th October, 2026 to be considered. Please include your cover letter and your CV highlighting how you can meet the skills and experiences detailed above. Please note we are happy to answer your questions but we do not accept applications by email. Equity The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Treaty of Waitangi and achieving equity outcomes for staff and students in a safe, inclusive, and equitable environment.\u00a0 For further information on services for M\u0101ori, Pacific, women, LGBTQIATakat\u0101pu iMVPFAFF + , people with disabilities, parenting support, flexible work and other personal circumstances go to \u00a0www.equity.auckland.ac.nz \u00a0 \n\n Waipapa Taumata Rau \u00a0 | The University of Auckland", "department": "Finance"}}, "rows": {"https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/TheUniversityOfAuckland/74